== Christian Practice

XII. Gracious and holy affections have their exercise and fruit in Christian practice.——I mean that they have such influence and power over the one who has them that they make a practice universally conformed to Christian rules, and directed by them, the practice and business of his life.

This implies three things. 1. That his behavior or practice in the world is universally conformed to Christian rules and directed by them. 2. That he makes a business of such a holy practice above all things: that it is a business he is chiefly engaged in and devoted to, and pursues with the highest earnestness and diligence, so that he may be said to make this practice of religion, in a special way, #emph[his work and business.] And 3. That he persists in it to the end of his life. So it may be said to be not only his business at certain seasons (the business of Sabbath days, or of certain extraordinary times, or the business of a month, or a year, or seven years, or his business under certain circumstances), but the #emph[business of his life:] the business he perseveres in through all changes and under all trials, as long as he lives.

The necessity of each of these in all true Christians is most clearly and fully taught in the word of God.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. It is necessary that men be universally obedient: #footnote[He who claims to be godly and turns aside to crooked ways is a hypocrite. For those who are really godly live in a way of obedience. Ps 119:1, 2, 3: “Blessed are those whose way is blameless, who walk in the Law of the #smallcaps[Lord]. … They do no iniquity.” Luke 1:6: “Both of them were righteous in the sight of God, walking blamelessly in all the commandments and decrees of the Lord.” But those who live in ways of sin are deceivers, for all such will be rejected in the day of judgment. Matt 7:23: “Depart from Me, you#super[+] workers of lawlessness!” We have the same in Luke 13:27. If men live in a way of disobedience, they do not love God, for love will make men keep God’s commandments. 1 John 5:3: “This is the love of God, that we keep His commandments. And His commandments are not burdensome.” If men live in a way of disobedience, they do not have a spirit of faith, for faith sanctifies men. Acts 26:18: “sanctified by faith in Me.” If men live in a way of disobedience, they are not Christ’s sheep, for his sheep hear his voice (John 10:27). Men who live in a way of disobedience are not born of God. 1 John 3:9: “Anyone born of God refuses to practice sin.” Men who live in a way of disobedience are the slaves of sin. John 8:34: “Everyone who sins is a slave to sin.”—A course of outward sin is an evidence of hypocrisy, whether it is a sin of omission or of commission. If men live in the neglect of known duties, or in the practice of known evils, that will be their condemnation, whatever the sin may be: profanity, impurity, lying or injustice.—If men allow themselves in malice, envy, lustful thoughts or profane thoughts, that will condemn them, even though those corruptions do not break out in any scandalous way. These thoughts are an evidence of a rotten heart. Titus 3:3: “At one time we too were foolish, disobedient, misled, and enslaved to all sorts of desires and pleasures—living in malice and envy, being hated and hating one another.” If a man allows himself in malice and envy, even though he thinks he does not, he is a hypocrite. Though his conscience disapproves of it, yet if his heart allows it, he is no saint.—Some make claims to godliness by which they deceive not only others but also (which is a great deal worse) themselves. But this will condemn them: that they live in a course of sin, and so must go with ungodly men. Ps 125:5: “Those who turn to crooked ways the #smallcaps[Lord] will banish with the evildoers.” If there is a great change in a man’s conduct, and he is reformed in several particular things, yet if there is one evil way, the man is an ungodly man. Where there is piety there is universal obedience. A man may have great weaknesses and yet be a godly man. So it was with #emph[Lot, David] and #emph[Peter.] But if he lives in a way of sin, he does not merely make his godliness doubtful; it is full evidence against him. Men who are godly have respect to all God’s commandments (Ps 119:6). There are a great many commands, and if there is one of them that a man has no respect for, he will be put to shame another day. If a man lives in one evil way, he is not subject to God’s authority, but lives in rebellion. That will take away all his pleas and cut off all his claims at once, and he will be condemned in the day of judgment.—One way of sin is objection enough against the man’s salvation, even though the sin he lives in is only small. Such persons will not be guilty of perjury, stealing, drunkenness or fornication. They look on these as heinous things, and they are afraid of them. But they do not much care if they cheat a little in a bargain, if they praise too highly a thing they are about to sell, if they break a promise, if they spend the Sabbath unprofitably, if they neglect secret prayer, if they talk rudely and insult others. They think these are only small things. If they can keep clear of great transgression, they hope that God will not insist on small things. But in fact all the commands of God are established by divine authority. A small shot may kill a man as well as a cannonball. A small leak may sink a ship. If a man lives in small sins, that shows he has no love to God, no sincere care to please and honor God. Little sins are of a damning nature as well as great ones. If they do not deserve as much punishment as greater ones, yet they do deserve damnation. There is a contempt of God in all sins. Matt 5:19: “Whoever breaks one of the least of these commandments and teaches others to do likewise will be called least in the kingdom of heaven.” Prov 19:16: “He who keeps a commandment preserves his soul, but he who despises his ways will die.” If a man says, “This is a great command,” and so lays weight on it, and “That is a little commandment,” and so does not regard it but will allow himself to break it, he is in a perishing condition. #emph[Stoddard’s] way to know sincerity and hypocrisy.] 1 John 3:3, etc.: “Everyone who has this hope in Him purifies himself, just as Christ is pure.—But you#super[+] know that Christ appeared to take away sins, and in Him there is no sin. No one who remains in Him keeps on sinning. No one who continues to sin has seen Him or known Him.—The one who practices righteousness is righteous, just as Christ is righteous. The one who practices sin is of the devil.” Chapter 5:18: “We know that anyone born of God does not keep on sinning; the one who was born of God keeps himself, and the evil one cannot touch him.” John 15:14: “You#super[+] are My friends if you#super[+] do what I command you#super[+].” Jas 2:10: “Whoever keeps the whole law but stumbles at just one point is guilty of breaking all of it.” 1 Cor 6:9: “Do you#super[+] not know that the wicked will not inherit the kingdom of God? Do not be deceived: Neither the sexually immoral, nor idolaters,” etc., “will inherit the kingdom of God.” Gal 5:19, 20: “The acts of the flesh are obvious: sexual immorality, impurity, and debauchery; idolatry and sorcery; hatred, discord, jealousy, and rage; rivalries, divisions, factions, and envy; drunkenness, orgies, and the like. I warn you#super[+], as I did before, that those who practice such things will not inherit the kingdom of God.” That is as much as to say, those who practice any sort of wickedness. Job 31:3, 4, 5, 6, 7: “Does not disaster come to the unjust and calamity to the workers of iniquity? Does He not see my ways and count my every step? … Let God weigh me with honest scales, that He may know my integrity. If my steps have turned from the path, if my heart has followed my eyes, or if impurity has stuck to my hands,” etc. Ezek 33:15: “If he … walks in the statutes of life without practicing iniquity, then he will surely live.” If only one member is corrupt and we do not cut it off, it will carry the whole body to hell (Matt 5:29, 30). #emph[Saul] was commanded to kill all God’s enemies, the #emph[Amalekites;] and he killed all but #emph[Agag,] and sparing his life proved to be his ruin. #emph[Caleb] and #emph[Joshua] entered into God’s promised rest because they #emph[wholly] followed the Lord (Num 14:24; and 32:11, 12; Deut 1:36; Josh 14:6, 8, 9, 14). #emph[Naaman]’s hypocrisy appeared in this: however greatly he seemed to be moved with gratitude to God for healing his leprosy, and though he pledged himself to serve him, yet in one thing he wanted to be excused. And #emph[Herod,] though he feared #emph[John] and kept him safe, and heard him gladly, and did many things, was yet condemned, because in one thing he would not listen to him: in parting with his beloved #emph[Herodias.] So it is necessary that men part with their dearest iniquities, which are like their right hand and right eye: the sins that most easily beset them, and that they are most exposed to by their natural inclinations, evil customs or particular circumstances, as well as with others. #emph[Joseph] would not make himself known to his brothers, who had sold him, until #emph[Benjamin,] the beloved child of the family, the one hardest to part with, was given up. In the same way Christ will not reveal his love to us until we part with our dearest lusts, and until we are brought to comply with the most difficult duties, and those we have the greatest aversion to.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And it is important to observe that, for a man to be truly said to be universally obedient, his obedience must not consist only in #emph[negatives,] or in universally avoiding wicked practices, sins of commission. He must also be universal in the #emph[positives] of religion. Sins of omission are as much breaches of God’s commands as sins of commission. In Matthew 25, Christ represents those on the left hand as condemned and cursed to everlasting fire for sins of omission: “I was hungry and you#super[+] gave Me nothing to eat,” etc. So a man cannot be said to be universally obedient, and of a Christian conduct, merely because he is no thief, no oppressor, no cheat, no drunkard, no tavern-haunter, no whoremonger, no rioter, no night-walker, not unclean, not profane in his language, no slanderer, no liar, not furious, not malicious, no reviler. He who goes this far and no farther is falsely said to be of a conduct that becomes the gospel. For this it is also necessary that he be of a serious, religious, devout, humble, meek, forgiving, peaceful, respectful, gracious to those beneath them, benevolent, merciful, charitable and beneficent walk and conduct. Without such things as these he does not obey the laws of Christ, laws that he and his apostles abundantly insisted on as of the greatest importance and necessity.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. For men to be true Christians, it is necessary that they pursue the business of religion and the service of God with great earnestness and diligence, as the work they devote themselves to and make the main business of their lives. All Christ’s #emph[own people] not only do good works but are #emph[zealous for good works] (Titus 2:14). No man can do the service of two masters at once. Those who are God’s true servants give themselves up to his service and make it, as it were, their whole work, employing their whole hearts and the chief of their strength in it. Phil 3:13: “One thing I do.” Christians in their effectual calling are not called to idleness, but to labor in God’s vineyard, and to spend their day doing a great and laborious service. All true Christians comply with this call (as is implied in its being an effectual call) and do the work of Christians. Everywhere in the New Testament this work is compared to those exercises in which men usually exert their strength with the greatest earnestness, such as running, wrestling and fighting. All true Christians are good and faithful soldiers of Jesus Christ, and #emph[fight the good fight of faith,] for none but those who do so ever #emph[take hold of eternal life.] Those who #emph[fight like men beating the air] never win the crown of victory. #emph[Those who run in a race all run, but only one wins the prize;] and those who are slack and negligent in their course do not #emph[run in such a way as to win it.] The kingdom of heaven is not to be taken except by violence. Without earnestness there is no getting along in that narrow way that leads to life, and so no arriving at the state of glorious life and happiness it leads to. Without earnest labor there is no climbing the steep and high hill of #emph[Zion,] and so no arriving at the heavenly city on its top. Without constant labor there is no stemming the swift stream we swim in, so as ever to come to the fountain of the water of life at its head. We need to #emph[watch and pray always,] in order to #emph[escape those] dreadful #emph[things] that are coming on the ungodly, and to #emph[be counted worthy to stand before the Son of Man.] We need to #emph[put on the whole armor of God, and to do everything to stand,] in order to avoid a total overthrow and being utterly destroyed by the #emph[flaming arrows of the devil.] We need to #emph[forget what is behind,] and to #emph[be straining toward what is ahead, and pressing on toward the goal for the prize of the high calling of God in Christ Jesus our Lord,] in order to obtain that prize. Slothfulness in the service of God, in his professed servants, is as damning as open rebellion. For the #emph[slothful servant] is a #emph[wicked servant,] and will be thrown into the outer darkness among God’s open enemies (Matt 25:26, 28). Those who are #emph[slothful] are not #emph[followers of those who through faith and patience inherit the promises.] Heb 6:11, 12: “We want each of you#super[+] to show this same diligence to the full assurance of hope to the very end. Then you#super[+] will not be slothful, but followers of those who through faith and patience inherit what has been promised.” And all who follow #emph[that cloud of witnesses] who have gone before to heaven #emph[throw off every weight and the sin that so easily besets them, and run with endurance the race set before them] (Heb 12:1). The true faith by which persons rely on the righteousness of Christ and the work he has done for them, and truly feed and live on him, is always accompanied by such a spirit of earnestness in the Christian work and course. This was typified of old by the way the children of #emph[Israel] fed on the Passover lamb. They were told to eat it like people in haste, with their loins girded, their shoes on their feet and their staff in their hand. Exod 12:11: “This is how you#super[+] are to eat it: You#super[+] must be fully dressed for travel, with your#super[+] sandals on your#super[+] feet and your#super[+] staff in your#super[+] hand. You#super[+] are to eat in haste; it is the #smallcaps[Lord]’s Passover.”]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. Every true Christian perseveres in this way of universal obedience, and of diligent and earnest service of God, through all the various kinds of trials he meets with, to the end of his life. That all true saints, all who obtain eternal life, persevere in this way in the practice of religion and the service of God is a doctrine so abundantly taught in Scripture that to list all the texts that imply it one by one would be endless. I will content myself with referring to some in the margin. #footnote[Deut 5:29. Deut 32:18, 19, 20. 1 Chr 28:9. Ps 78:7, 8, 10, 11, 35, 36, 37, 41, 42, 56, #emph[etc.] Ps 106:3, 12, 13, 14, 15. Ps 125:4, 5. Prov 26:11. Isa 64:5. Jer 17:13. Ezek 3:20. #emph[And] 18. 24. #emph[And] 33. 12, 13. Matt 10:22. Matt 13:4,—8, with verses 19,—23. Matt 25:8. Matt 24:12, 13. Luke 9:62. #emph[Chap.] 12. 35, #emph[etc. Chap.] 22. 28. #emph[Chap.] 17. 32. John 8:30, 31. #emph[Chap:] 15. 6, 7, 8, 10, 16. #emph[Rom:] 2. 7. #emph[Chap.] 11. 22. Col 1:22, 23. Heb 3:6, 12, 14. #emph[Chap.] 6. 11, 12. #emph[Chap.] 10. 35, #emph[etc.] Jas 1:25. Rev 2:13, 26. #emph[Chap.] 2. 10. 1 Tim 2:15. 2 Tim 4:4, 5, 6, 7, 8.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But #emph[that] part of perseverance in obedience which Scripture chiefly insists on as a special mark of the truth of grace is that professing Christians continue in the practice of their duty, and stay steadfast in a holy walk, through the various #emph[trials] they meet with.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[By #emph[trials] I mean here the things that occur, and that a professing Christian meets with in his course, which especially make his continuing in his duty and faithfulness to God hard for nature. Again and again Scripture calls these things #emph[trials] or #emph[temptations] (words that mean the same thing). They are of various kinds. Many things make it hard for persons to continue in the way of their duty because they tend to cherish and feed, or to stir up and provoke, their lusts and corruptions. Many things make it hard to continue in the way of duty because they are alluring and tend to entice persons to sin, or because they tend to remove restraints and make them bold in iniquity. Other things try the soundness and steadfastness of professing Christians because they tend to make their duty look terrible to them, and so to frighten and drive them away from it: such as the sufferings their duty will expose them to, pain, ill will, contempt and reproach, or the loss of outward possessions and comforts. If persons, after they have made a profession of religion, live any considerable time in this world, so full of changes and so full of evil, they cannot help meeting with many trials of their sincerity and steadfastness. Besides, it is God’s way in his providence to bring trials on his professing friends and servants on purpose, so that he may make them manifest, and may set before their own consciences, and often before the world, sufficient matter to show convincingly the state they are in. This appears from innumerable Scriptures; some are referred to in the margin. #footnote[Gen 22:1. Exod 15:25. #emph[Chap.] 16. 4. Deut 8:2, 15, 16. #emph[Chap.] 13. 3: #emph[Judges] 2. 22. #emph[Chap.] 3. 1, 4. Job 23:10. Ps 66:10, 11. Ezek 3:20. Dan 12:10. Zech 13:9. Matt 8:19, 20. #emph[Chap.] 18. 21, 22. Luke 1:35. 1 Cor 11:19. 2 Cor 8:8. Jas 1:12. 1 Pet 4:12. 1 John 2:19. Heb 11:17. Rev 3:10.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[True saints may be guilty of some kinds and degrees of backsliding. They may be overcome by particular temptations, and may fall into sin, indeed into great sins. But they can never fall away so far as to grow weary of religion and the service of God, and habitually to dislike it and neglect it, whether for its own sake or because of the difficulties that come with it. This is evident from Galatians 6:9; Rom 2:7; Heb 10:36; Isa 43:22; Mal 1:13. They can never backslide so far as to continue no longer in a #emph[way] of universal obedience, or so that it ceases to be their #emph[manner] to observe all the rules of Christianity and do all the duties required, even the most difficult, and in the most difficult circumstances. #footnote[One way of sin is objection enough against men’s salvation, even though their temptations are great. Some persons delight in iniquity; they take pleasure in rudeness and in intemperate practices. But there are others who do not delight in sin. When they can conveniently avoid it, they do not choose it; unless they are under some great necessity, they will not do it. They are afraid to sin; they think it is dangerous, and take some care to avoid it. But at times they force themselves to sin. They are brought into difficulties and cannot see how to avoid it well; it seems a dangerous thing not to do it. If #emph[Naaman] does not bow down in the house of #emph[Rimmon,] the king will be in a rage with him and take away his office, perhaps take away his life; and so he complies (2 Kgs 5:18).—So #emph[Jeroboam] forced himself to set up the calves at #emph[Dan] and #emph[Bethel.] He thought that if the people went up to #emph[Jerusalem] to worship, they would return to #emph[Rehoboam] and kill him, so he must think of some way to deliver himself in this strait (1 Kgs 12:27, 28).—He was driven by what seemed necessity to take this wicked course. So the stony-ground hearers were willing to keep the profession of the true religion, but the case was such that they thought they could not well do it. Matt 13:21: “When trouble or persecution comes because of the word, he quickly falls away.”—So #emph[Achan] and #emph[Gehazi] had unusual opportunities to get an estate. If they lived twenty years they were not likely to have such an advantage again; so they force themselves to stretch a point and break the law of God. They lay a necessity on estate and liberty and life, but not on obedience. If a man is willing to serve God in ordinary cases, but excuses himself when there are great difficulties, he is not godly. It is a small matter to serve God when men have no temptation. But #emph[Lot] was holy in #emph[Sodom,] and #emph[Noah] was righteous in the old world. Temptations try men, but they do not force men to sin; and grace will establish the heart in a day of temptation. Those who endure temptation are blessed (Jas 1:12), but those who fall away in a day of temptation are cursed. #emph[Stoddard’s] way to know sincerity and hypocrisy.] This is abundantly clear from the things already observed. Nor can they ever fall away so far as to be habitually more engaged in other things than in the business of religion, or so that it becomes their way and manner to serve something else more than God, or so as to cease, as a settled thing, to serve God with such earnestness and diligence that they are still habitually devoted and given up to the business of religion. This cannot be, unless those words of Christ can fall to the ground, “No one can serve two masters,” and those of the apostle, “Whoever chooses to be a friend of the world renders himself an enemy of God”; and unless a saint can change his God and still be a true saint. Nor can a true saint ever fall away so far that it comes to this: that ordinarily there is no remarkable difference between his walk and behavior since his conversion and what it was before. Those who are truly converted are new men, new creatures: new not only within but without. They are sanctified throughout, in spirit, soul and body. Old things have passed away; all things have become new. They have new hearts, and new eyes, new ears, new tongues, new hands, new feet, that is, a new conduct and practice. They walk in newness of life, and continue to do so to the end of life. And as for those who fall away and visibly cease to do so, it is a sign they never were risen with Christ. #footnote[From this we learn what verdict to pass and give concerning those men who decay and fall away from the Lord. They never had oil in the vessel; they never had a dram of grace in their heart. So 1 John 2:19: “For if they had belonged to us, they would have remained with us.” It seems they were men so eminent and excellent that there were no brands or marks on them to warn the churches that they were marked out for apostasy; they were found to be unsound only by their apostasy. And this was argument enough. #emph[Shepard]’s parable, Part I. P. 226.] And especially when men’s opinion that they are converted, and so in a safe state, is the very cause of their coming to this, it is a most evident sign of their hypocrisy. #footnote[When a man’s rising is the cause of his fall, or seals him up in his fall, or at least is the cause of it through his corruption. #emph[For example,] there was a time when a man lived a loose, careless, carnal life. By the ministry of some word, or the reading of some book, or speaking with some friend, he comes to be convinced of his misery and woeful condition, and sees no good or grace in himself; he has been deceived up to this time. At last he comes to get some light, some taste, some sorrows, some heart to use the means, some comfort and mercy and hope of life. And when it is like this with him, now he falls; he grows full and falls, and this rising is the cause of his fall. His light is darkness and death to him, and grows into a form of knowledge. His rising makes him fall into formality, and then into profaneness. So his tasting satisfies him; his sorrows empty his heart of sorrow for sin; his sorrows for his falls harden his heart in his falls; and all the means of recovering him harden him.—It is as it is in diseases: if the medicine and the food turn to poison, then there is no hope of recovery; the man is sick to death now. The saint’s little measure makes him forget what is behind. #emph[Shepard]’s parable, Part I. P. 226.] And this holds whether their falling away is into their former sins or into some new kind of wickedness, the corruption of nature only turned into a new channel instead of being mortified. So it is when persons who think themselves converted, though they do not return to their former profaneness and lewdness, yet, from the high opinion they have of their experiences, graces and privileges, gradually settle more and more into a self-righteous and spiritually proud temper of mind, and into the kind of behavior and conduct that naturally arises from it. When it is like this with men, however far they may seem to be from their former evil practices, this alone is enough to condemn them, and may make their last state far worse than the first. For this seems to be the very case of the #emph[Jews] of that generation whom Christ speaks of in Matt 12:43, 44, 45. They had been awakened by #emph[John the Baptist]’s preaching and brought to a reformation of their former licentious ways, by which the unclean spirit was, as it were, turned out, and the house swept and put in order. Yet, being #emph[empty] of God and of grace, they became full of themselves. They were lifted up in an exceedingly high opinion of their own righteousness and eminent holiness, and grew used to a self-exalting behavior to match. So they exchanged the sins of tax collectors and prostitutes for those of the Pharisees, and in the end had seven devils worse than the first.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[So I have explained what exercise and fruit I mean when I say that gracious affections have their exercise and fruit in Christian practice.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The reason gracious affections have such a tendency and effect appears from many things already observed in the earlier parts of this discourse.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The reason for it appears from this: that #emph[gracious affections arise from operations and influences that are spiritual,] and that the inward principle they flow from is something #emph[divine:] a communication of God, a participation of the divine nature, Christ living in the heart, the Holy Spirit dwelling there in union with the faculties of the soul, as an inward vital principle, exerting his own proper nature in the exercise of those faculties. This is enough to show us why true grace should have such activity, power and efficacy. No wonder that what is divine is powerful and effectual, for it has omnipotence on its side. If God dwells in the heart and is vitally united to it, he will show that he is God by the efficacy of his working. Christ is not in the heart of a saint as in a tomb, or as a dead Savior who does nothing, but as in his temple, and as one who is alive from the dead. For in the heart where Christ savingly is, there he lives, and exerts himself according to the power of that endless life he received at his resurrection. So every saint who has the benefit of Christ’s sufferings is made to know and experience the power of his resurrection. The Spirit of Christ, who is the immediate spring of grace in the heart, is all life, all power, all act. 1 Cor 2:4: “with a demonstration of the Spirit’s power.” 1 Thess 1:5: “Our gospel came to you#super[+] not only in word, but also in power, in the Holy Spirit.” 1 Cor 4:20: “For the kingdom of God is not a matter of talk but of power.” That is why saving affections, though often they do not make as great a noise and show as others, yet have in them a hidden solidity, life and strength. By it they take hold of the heart and carry it away, leading it into a kind of captivity (2 Cor 10:5), and gain a full and steadfast determination of the will for God and holiness. Ps 110:3: “Your people shall be willing in the day of Your power.” And this is how holy affections have a governing power in the course of a man’s life. A statue may look very much like a real man, and a handsome man. Indeed, to the eye it may resemble a very lively, strong and active man. But it lacks an inward principle of life and strength, and so it does nothing, it brings nothing about; there is no action or working to match the show. False discoveries and affections do not go deep enough to reach and govern the spring of men’s actions and practice. The seed on stony ground had no depth of soil, and the root did not go deep enough to bring forth fruit. But gracious affections go to the very bottom of the heart and take hold of the very innermost springs of life and activity. It is chiefly in this that the power of true godliness appears: in its being effectual in practice. And the efficacy of godliness in this respect is what the apostle has in view when he speaks of the power of godliness (2 Tim 3:5). This is very plain, for he is declaring there in particular how some professing Christians would notoriously fail in the practice of religion; and then in the 5th verse he observes that in being of such an unholy practice they deny the power of godliness, though they have the form of it. It is true that the power of godliness is exerted first of all within the soul, in the felt, lively exercise of gracious affections there. Yet the chief evidence of this power of godliness is in those exercises of holy affections that are practical, and in their being practical: in conquering the will, and conquering the lusts and corruptions of men, and carrying men on in the way of holiness through all temptation, difficulty and opposition.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Again, the reason gracious affections have their exercise and effect in Christian practice appears from this (which has also been observed before): that #emph[the first objective ground of gracious affections is the transcendently excellent and lovely nature of divine things as they are in themselves, and not any supposed relation they bear to self or self-interest.] This shows why holy affections will make men holy in their practice universally. What makes men partial in religion is that they seek themselves, and not God, in their religion, and take up religion not for its own excellent nature, but only to serve a purpose. He who takes up religion only to serve a purpose will take up no more of it than he imagines serves that purpose. But he who takes up religion for its own excellent and lovely nature takes up all that has that nature. He who embraces religion for its own sake embraces the whole of religion. This also shows why gracious affections will make men practice religion perseveringly and at all times. In the course of time religion may change greatly, in many respects, in how well it agrees with men’s private interest. So he who complies with it only from selfish views is liable to forsake it when times change. But the excellent nature of religion, as it is in itself, does not vary. It is always the same, at all times and through all changes; it never alters in any respect.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The reason gracious affections issue in holy practice appears further from the kind of excellence of divine things that, as has been observed, is the foundation of all holy affection, #emph[namely, their moral excellence, or the beauty of their holiness.] No wonder that a love to holiness for holiness’ sake inclines persons to practice holiness, and to practice everything that is holy. Since holiness is the main thing that stirs, draws and governs all gracious affections, no wonder that all such affections tend to holiness. What men love, they desire to have, to be united to and to possess. The beauty men delight in, they desire to be adorned with. The acts men delight in, they are necessarily inclined to do.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And what has been observed of that #emph[divine teaching and leading of the Spirit of God] which there is in gracious affections shows the reason for this tendency of such affections to a universally holy practice. For, as has been observed, the Spirit of God in this divine teaching and leading of his gives the soul a natural relish of the sweetness of what is holy, and of everything that is holy, so far as it comes into view, and stirs up a distaste and disgust for everything that is unholy.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The same also appears from what has been observed of the nature of the #emph[spiritual knowledge] that is the foundation of all holy affection: that it consists in #emph[a sense and view of that excellence in divine things which is supreme and transcendent.] For by this, these things appear worthy above all others to be chosen and held to. By the sight of the transcendent glory of Christ, true Christians see him to be worthy to be followed, and so are powerfully drawn after him. They see him to be worthy that they should forsake all for him. By the sight of that surpassing loveliness they are thoroughly disposed to be subject to him, and committed to labor with earnestness and activity in his service, and made willing to go through all difficulties for his sake. And it is the discovery of this divine excellence of Christ that makes them constant to him. For it makes so deep an impression on their minds that they cannot forget him. They will follow him wherever he goes, and it is useless for anyone to try to draw them away from him.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The reason for this practical tendency and issue of gracious affections appears further from what has been observed of such affections being #emph[accompanied by a thorough conviction of the judgment of the reality and certainty of divine things.] No wonder that those who were never thoroughly convinced that there is any reality in the things of religion will never go to the labor and trouble of such an earnest, universal and persevering practice of religion, through all difficulties, self-denials and sufferings, depending on something they are not convinced of. But on the other hand, those who are thoroughly convinced of the certain truth of those things must be governed by them in their practice. For the things revealed in the word of God are so great, and so infinitely more important than all other things, that it is inconsistent with human nature for a man fully to believe their truth and not be influenced by them above all things in his practice.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Again, the reason for this expression and effect of holy affections in practice appears from what has been observed of #emph[a change of nature that accompanies such affections.] Without a change of nature, men’s practice will not be thoroughly changed. Until the tree is made good, the fruit will not be good. Men do not gather grapes from thorns, or figs from thistles. The pig may be washed, and look clean for a little while, but without a change of nature it will still wallow in the mire. Nature is a more powerful principle of action than anything that opposes it. Though it may be forcibly restrained for a while, it will in the end overcome what restrains it. It is like the stream of a river: it may be stopped for a while with a dam, but if nothing is done to dry up the spring, it will not be stopped forever; it will find a course, either in its old channel or a new one. Nature is something more constant and lasting than any of the things on which carnal men’s reformation and righteousness are founded. When a natural man denies his lust, and lives a strict, religious life, and seems humble, painstaking and earnest in religion, it is not natural; it is all a force against nature, as when a stone is thrown violently upward. That force will gradually be spent, but nature will remain in its full strength, and so it prevails again, and the stone comes back down. As long as corrupt nature is not mortified, but the principle is left whole in a man, it is useless to expect that it will not govern. But if the old nature is truly mortified, and a new and heavenly nature infused, then it may well be expected that men will walk in newness of life, and continue to do so to the end of their days.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The reason for this practical exercise and effect of holy affections may also be partly seen from what has been said of the #emph[spirit of humility that accompanies them.] A spirit of obedience consists very much in humility. A proud spirit is a rebellious spirit, but a humble spirit is a yielding, submissive, obedient spirit. We see among men that a servant who is of a haughty spirit is not inclined to be submissive and obedient to his master’s will in everything; but it is otherwise with a servant who is of a lowly spirit.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And the #emph[lamblike, dovelike spirit] that has been spoken of, which accompanies all gracious affections, fulfills (as the apostle observes, Rom 13:8, 9, 10, and Gal 5:14) all the duties of the second table of the law. Christian practice consists very much in these duties, and the outward practice of Christianity chiefly consists in them.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And the reason gracious affections are accompanied by the strict, universal and constant obedience that has been spoken of appears further from what has been observed of the #emph[tenderness of spirit] that accompanies the affections of true saints. It gives them so quick and lively a sense of pain at the presence of moral evil, and such a dread of the appearance of evil.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And one great reason the Christian practice that flows from gracious affections is universal, constant and persevering appears from what has been observed of those affections themselves, from which this practice flows: that they are universal and constant, in all kinds of holy exercises, toward all objects, in all circumstances and at all seasons, #emph[in a beautiful symmetry and proportion.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And much of the reason holy affections are expressed and shown in such earnestness, activity, commitment and perseverance in holy practice as has been spoken of appears from what has been observed of the spiritual appetite and longing for further attainments in religion, which always accompanies true affection, and does not decay but increases as those affections increase.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[So we see how the tendency of holy affections to such a Christian practice as has been explained appears from each of the characteristics of holy affection spoken of before.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And this point may be further illustrated and confirmed if we consider where the holy Scriptures abundantly place sincerity and soundness in religion. They place it in making a full choice of God as our only Lord and portion, forsaking all for him; in a full determination of the will for God and Christ, after counting the cost; in our hearts taking up and complying with the religion of Jesus Christ, with all that belongs to it, embracing it with all its difficulties, as it were hating our dearest earthly enjoyments, and even our own lives, for Christ; in giving up ourselves, with all that we have, wholly and forever, to Christ, without keeping anything back or making any reserve; or, in one word, in the great duty of #emph[self-denial] for Christ, that is, in denying, as it were disowning and renouncing, ourselves for him, making ourselves nothing so that he may be all. See the texts to this effect referred to in the margin. #footnote[Matt 5:29, 30. Matt 6:24. #emph[Chap.] 8. 19,—22. #emph[Chap.] 4. 18,—22. #emph[Chap.] 10. 37, 38, 39. #emph[Chap.] 13. 44, 45, 46. #emph[Chap.] 16. 24, 25, 26. #emph[Chap.] 18. 8, 9. #emph[Chap.] 19. 21, 27,—29. Luke 5:27, 28. Luke 10:42. #emph[Chap.] 12. 33, 34. #emph[Chap.] 14. 16,—20, 25,—33. #emph[Chap.] 16. 13. Acts 4:34, 35. With #emph[chap.] 5. 1,—11. Rom 6:3,—8. Gal 2:20. #emph[Chap.] 6. 14. Phil 3:7,—10. Jas 1:8, 9, 10. #emph[Chap.] 4. 4. 1 John 2:15. Rev 14:4. Gen 12:1,—4. With Heb 11:8, 9, 10. Gen 22:12. And Heb 11:17. Heb 11:24,—27. Deut 13:6. And #emph[chap.] 33. 9. Ruth 1:6,—16. With Ps 45:10, 11. And 2 Sam 15:19,—22. Ps 73:25. Ps 16:5, 6. Lam 3:24. Jer 10:16.] Now surely, having a heart to forsake all for Christ tends to actually forsaking all for him, so far as there is occasion and we are put to the trial. And having a heart to deny ourselves for Christ tends to denying ourselves in deed when Christ and self-interest stand in competition. Giving up ourselves, with all that we have, in our hearts, without making any reserve there, tends to our behaving universally as his, as subject to his will and devoted to his ends. Our hearts entirely taking up the religion of Jesus, with all that belongs to it and with all the difficulties that come with it, after deliberately counting the cost, tends to a universal taking up of the same in act and deed: to actually going through all the difficulties we meet with in the way of religion, and so holding out with patience and perseverance.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The tendency of grace in the heart to holy practice is very direct, and the connection most natural, close and necessary. True grace is not an inactive thing. There is nothing in heaven or earth of a more active nature, for it is life itself, and the most active kind of life, spiritual and divine life. It is no barren thing. There is nothing in the universe that by its nature has a greater tendency to fruit. Godliness in the heart has as direct a relation to practice as a fountain has to a stream, or as the luminous nature of the sun has to the beams it sends out, or as life has to breathing, or the beating of the pulse, or any other vital act; or as a habit or principle of action has to action. For it is the very nature and idea of grace that it is a principle of holy action or practice. Regeneration, the work of God in which grace is infused, has a direct relation to practice. For practice is the very end of it, the end in view of which the whole work is done. In this mighty and manifold change worked in the soul, everything is designed and framed so as to tend directly to this end. Eph 2:10: “For we are God’s workmanship, created in Christ Jesus to do good works.” Indeed, it is the very end of the redemption of Christ. Titus 2:14: “He gave Himself for us to redeem us from all lawlessness and to purify for Himself a people for His own possession, zealous for good deeds.” 2 Cor 5:15: “He died for all, that those who live should no longer live for themselves, but for Him who died for them and was raised again.” Heb 9:14: “How much more will the blood of Christ, who through the eternal Spirit offered Himself unblemished to God, purify our consciences from works of death, so that we may serve the living God!” Col 1:21, 22: “Once you#super[+] were alienated from God and were hostile in your#super[+] minds, engaging in evil deeds. But now He has reconciled you#super[+] by Christ’s physical body through death to present you#super[+] holy, unblemished, and blameless in His presence.” 1 Pet 1:18: “For you#super[+] know that it was not with perishable things such as silver or gold that you#super[+] were redeemed from the empty way of life”—. Luke 1:74, 75: “deliverance from hostile hands, that we may serve Him without fear, in holiness and righteousness before Him all the days of our lives.” God often speaks of holy practice as the end of that great redemption that was a type, the redemption from #emph[Egyptian] bondage, as in Exod 4:23: “Let My son go so that he may serve Me.” So also ch. 4:23, and 7:16, and 8:1, 20, and 9:1, 13, and 10:3. And this is also declared to be the end of election. John 15:16: “You#super[+] did not choose Me, but I chose you#super[+]. And I appointed you#super[+] to go and bear fruit—fruit that will remain.” Eph 1:4: “For He chose us in Him before the foundation of the world to be holy and blameless in His presence. In love …” Chapter 2:10: “created … to do good works, which God prepared in advance as our way of life.” Holy practice is as much the end of all God does with his saints as fruit is the end of all the farmer does for the growth of his field or vineyard, as Scripture often represents the matter: Matt 3:10; chapter 13:8, 23, 24–30, 38; chapter 21:19, 33, 34; Luke 13:6; John 15:1, 2, 4, 5, 6, 8; 1 Cor 3:9; Heb 6:7, 8; Isa 5:1–8; Song 8:11, 12; Isa 27:2, 3. #footnote[To profess to know much is easy; but to bring your affections into subjection, to wrestle with lusts, to cross your wills and yourselves on every occasion, this is hard. The Lord looks for us to be serviceable to him in our lives and useful to men. What is within, the Lord and our brothers are never the better for; but the outward obedience flowing from it glorifies God and does good to men. The Lord will have this done. What else is the end of our planting and watering, but that the trees may be filled with sap? And what is the end of that sap, but that the trees may bring forth fruit? What does the farmer care for leaves and barren trees? Dr. #emph[Preston] of the Church’s carriage.] And so everything in a true Christian is designed to reach this end. This fruit of holy practice is what every grace, and every discovery, and every single thing that belongs to Christian experience, has a direct tendency to. #footnote[What is the end of every grace, but to soften the heart and make it pliable to some command or other? Look how many commandments there are: so many graces there are in virtue and efficacy, although not so many separate names are given them. The end of every such grace is to make us obedient. So the end of temperance is chastity, to bow the heart to these commands: “Be sober-minded,” etc., “not in sexual immorality and debauchery,” etc. When the Lord commands us not to be angry with our brother, the end of meekness, and the reason the Lord infuses it, is to keep us from thoughtless, rash anger. So with faith: its end is to take Jesus Christ, to make us obedient to the command of the gospel, which commands us to believe in him. So all graces join together only to frame and fashion the soul to obedience. Then as much obedience as there is in your lives, so much grace is there in your hearts, and no more. Therefore ask your hearts how subject you are to the Lord in your lives. It was the counsel #emph[Francis Spira] gave to those around him. “Learn from me, all of you,” he said, “to take heed of separating faith and obedience. I taught justification by faith, but neglected obedience; and that is why this has come upon me.” I have known some godly men whose comfort on their deathbeds came not from the inward acts of their minds, which, considered by themselves, might be open to misunderstanding, but from the course of obedience in their lives that issued from them. Let Christians see to it in all their conduct, in every relation they stand in, as scholars, tradesmen, husbands, wives; let them see to this, that when they come to die, they have been subject in all things. This will give comfort. Dr. #emph[Preston]’s Church’s carriage.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The constant and unbreakable connection between a Christian principle and profession in the true saints and the fruit of holy practice in their lives was foreshadowed of old in the make of the golden lampstand in the temple. It is beyond doubt that the golden lampstand, with its seven branches and seven lamps, was a type of the church of Christ. The Holy Spirit himself has been pleased to put the matter beyond doubt by representing his church by such a golden lampstand with seven lamps in the fourth chapter of #emph[Zechariah,] and by representing the seven churches of #emph[Asia] by seven golden lampstands in the first chapter of #emph[Revelation.] That golden lampstand in the temple was made everywhere, throughout its whole frame, with #emph[knops and flowers] (Exod 25:31 to the end, and ch. 37:17–24). The word translated #emph[knop] means in the original an apple or pomegranate. There was #emph[a knop and a flower, a knop and a flower:] wherever there was a flower, there was an apple or pomegranate with it. The flower and the fruit were constantly joined, without fail. The flower contained the principles of the fruit, and a beautiful, promising appearance of it; and it never was a deceptive appearance. The principle or show of fruit always had real fruit accompanying it or following it. So it is in the church of Christ. There is the principle of fruit in grace in the heart. There is a lovely profession, signified by the open flowers of the lampstand. And there is fruit to match, in holy practice, constantly accompanying this principle and profession. Every branch of the golden lampstand, made up in this way of golden apples and flowers, was crowned on its top with a burning, shining lamp. For it is by this means that the saints shine as lights in the world: by making a fair and good profession of religion, and having their profession always joined with fruit to match it in practice. This agrees with our Savior’s words in Matt 5:15, 16: “Neither do people light a lamp and put it under a basket. Instead, they set it on a stand, and it gives light to everyone in the house. In the same way, let your#super[+] light shine before men, that they may #emph[see your#super[+] good works] and glorify your#super[+] Father in heaven.” A fair and beautiful profession and golden fruits accompanying one another are the lovely ornaments of the true church of Christ. That is why we find that apples and flowers were the ornaments not only of the lampstand in the temple, but of the temple itself, which is a type of the church, which the apostle tells us #emph[is the temple of the living God.] See 1 Kgs 6:18: “The cedar paneling inside the temple was carved with knops and open flowers.” The ornaments and crown of the pillars at the entrance of the temple were of the same sort: they were lilies and pomegranates, that is, flowers and fruits mixed together (1 Kgs 7:18, 19). So it is with all those who are #emph[as pillars in the temple of God, who will never again go out,] or never be thrown out as intruders, as it is with all true saints. Rev 3:12: “The one who overcomes I will make a pillar in the temple of My God, and he will never again leave it.”]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Much the same thing seems to be signified by the ornaments on the skirt of the ephod, the garment of #emph[Aaron] the high priest, which were golden bells and pomegranates. That these skirts of #emph[Aaron]’s garment represent the church, or the saints (who are, as it were, the garment of Christ), is plain, for they are evidently spoken of in this way in Ps 133:1, 2: “Behold, how good and pleasant it is when brothers live together in harmony! It is like fine oil on the head, running down on the beard, running down #emph[Aaron]’s beard to the skirts of his robes.” That ephod of #emph[Aaron] signified the same thing as the seamless coat of Christ, our great high priest. As Christ’s coat had no seam but was woven in one piece from the top, so it was with the ephod (Exod 39:22). As God took care in his providence that Christ’s coat should not be torn, so God took special care that the ephod should not be torn (Exod 28:32, and ch. 39:23). The golden bells on this ephod, by their precious material and pleasant sound, well represent the good profession the saints make, and the pomegranates the fruit they bring forth. And in the hem of the ephod bells and pomegranates were constantly joined, as is observed again and again: there was #emph[a golden bell and a pomegranate, a golden bell and a pomegranate] (Exod 28:34, and ch. 39:26). So it is in the true saints: their good profession and their good fruit constantly accompany one another. The fruit they bring forth in their lives always answers to the pleasant sound of their profession.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Again, the very same thing is represented by Christ in his description of his bride, Song 7:2: “Your waist is a mound of wheat encircled by the lilies.” Here again are beautiful flowers and good fruit accompanying one another. The lilies were fair and beautiful flowers, and the wheat was good fruit.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[As this fruit of Christian practice is always found in true saints, as they have opportunity and trial, so it is found in them only. None but true Christians live such an obedient life, so universally devoted to their duty and given up to the business of a Christian, as has been explained. All unsanctified men are #emph[workers of iniquity.] They are of their father the devil, and the lusts of their father they will do. There is no hypocrite who will go through with the business of religion, and both begin and finish the tower. They will not endure the trials God usually brings on those who profess religion, but will turn aside to their crooked ways. They will not be thoroughly faithful to Christ in their practice and follow him wherever he goes. Whatever lengths they may go in religion in some instances, and though they may appear exceedingly strict and mightily engaged in the service of God for a season, yet they are servants of sin. The chains of their old taskmasters are not broken; their lusts still have a reigning power in their hearts; and so to these masters they will bow down again. #footnote[There is no unregenerate man, however far he goes and however much he does, who does not live in some one sin or other, secret or open, little or great. #emph[Judas] went far, but he was covetous. #emph[Herod] went far, but he loved his #emph[Herodias.] Every dog has his kennel, every pig has his swill, and every wicked man his lust. #emph[Shepard’s] sincere convert, 1st edition, p. 96. There is not one unsound heart in the world but, as they say of witches that they have some familiar spirit that sucks them, so they have some lust that is beloved by them; there is some beloved one to whom they have given a promise never to forsake it. #emph[Shepard]’s parable, Part I. P. 15. There is no man married to the law whose fig leaves do not cover some nakedness. All his duties always brood some lust. There is some one sin or other the man lives in, which either the Lord shows him and he will not part with, as with the young man, or else is so spiritual that he cannot see it all his lifetime. Read through the strictest of them all, and see this (Matt 23): “whitewashed tombs.” #emph[Paul,] who was blameless, yet (Eph 2:3; Titus 3:3) was “enslaved to all sorts of desires and pleasures.” And the reason is that the law is not the ministry of the Spirit (2 Cor 3:8, 9), which breaks a man off from every sin. There is no law that can give life (Gal 3:21). And this is why many men make strong resolutions and break them all again. This is why men sin and sorrow, and pray again, and then go on with more ease in their sin. Examine yourself: is there any living lust alongside your righteousness? Then it is certain that it is righteousness you are married to, and you were never yet matched to Christ. #emph[Shepard]’s parable, Part I. P. 19, 20. There is no hypocrite, though he takes up with Christ and for a time grows in knowledge of and communion with Christ, who does not have at that time hidden lusts and thorns that overgrow his growth and choke everything at last; and in the end he seeks to make a league between Christ and his lusts, and to reconcile them together. #emph[Shepard]’s parable, Part I. P. 109. —Their faith is in such a person as was never yet thoroughly torn away from sin. And here is the great wound of the most cunning hypocrites living.——Let a man be cast down as low as hell with sorrow, and lie under chains, quaking in dread of terror to come. Let a man then be raised up to heaven in joy, so that he is not able to live. Let a man reform and shine like an angel on earth. Yet if he is not torn away from lust, so that either you never saw it, or, if you did, you have not followed the Lord to remove it, but are proud, surly, worldly and sluggish still, false in your dealings, cunning in your trading, devils in your families, images in your churches, then you are objects of pity now, and will be objects of terror at the great day. For where sin remains in power, it will bring faith, and Christ, and joy into bondage and service to itself. #emph[Shepard]’s parable Part I. P. 125. It seems to me that it is with the best hypocrites as it is with various old merchants. They prize and desire the gain of trade, but to take the trouble to prepare the ship, to put themselves to the hazards and dangers of the ship, to go and fetch the treasure they prize, this they will never do. So many prize and earnestly desire the treasures of heaven. But to take the trouble of a voyage to heaven to fetch this treasure, to pass through the valley of #emph[Baca,] through tears, temptations, the powers of darkness, the breaches, opposition and contradictions of a sinful, unbelieving heart, through good report and evil report, to pass from one depth and wave to another: in this the best hypocrite fails, and so loses all at last. And I take this to be one of the great differences between the strong desires and esteem of hypocrites and of saints.—It is as it is with men who have two trades or two shops: one is as much as they can ever follow or tend, so at last they are forced to give up one, and they must neglect one. So it is here.—The spirit of sloth and slumber that the Lord always leaves the best hypocrite to so mightily oppresses all their senses that they cannot use all means effectually to accomplish their ends. And so a man desires the end but does not have it (Prov 13:4). #emph[Shepard]’s parable, Part I. P. 150, 151. Read through all of Scripture: constantly there were never any hypocrites who did not have this brand (Matt 7:23), “you#super[+] workers of iniquity.” #emph[Shepard]’s parable, Part I. P. 195. A carnal man may hit upon some good duty God commands, and refrain from some sin God forbids; but to go all the way through, he cannot. To take up reproach and disgrace, to lose his credit, to forsake his friends, to lose honor and riches and pleasures: this he will not do until he is humbled. Dr. #emph[Preston,] on #emph[Paul]’s conversion. So it is with men, because they lack humiliation. That is why their #emph[profession] and #emph[they] do not continue together, but part from each other willingly. They will do some things, but not all things; and they will give up some things, but not all things. And that is why our Savior says in Luke 14, “Whoever will not forsake all for My sake is not worthy of Me.” He is not worth saving who does not prize me above all things whatever. And a man will not prize Christ, or forsake all things for Christ, until he is humbled. Ibid.] Dan 12:10: “Many will be purified, made spotless, and refined, but the wicked will continue to act wickedly. None of the wicked will understand.” Isa 26: “Though grace is shown to the wicked man, he does not learn righteousness. In the land of righteousness he acts unjustly.” Isa 35:8: “And there will be a highway called the Way of Holiness. The unclean will not travel it.” Hos 14:9: “The ways of the #smallcaps[Lord] are right, and the righteous walk in them but the rebellious stumble in them.” Job 27:8, 9, 10: “What is the hope of the hypocrite …? Will he delight in the Almighty? Will he call upon God at all times?” An unsanctified man may hide his sin, and may in many things and for a season refrain from sin. But he will not be brought finally to renounce his sin and give it a bill of divorce. Sin is too dear to him for him to be willing to do that. “Evil is sweet in his mouth,” and so “he conceals it under his tongue”; “he cannot bear to let it go and keeps it in his mouth” (Job 20:12, 13). It is chiefly in this that the narrowness of the gate and of the way that leads to life consists, on account of which carnal men will not go in by it: namely, that it is a way of utterly denying and finally renouncing all ungodliness, and so a way of #emph[self-denial] or #emph[self-renunciation.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Many natural men, under the means used with them and God’s strivings with them to bring them to forsake their sins, treat their sins as #emph[Pharaoh] treated his pride and covetousness, which he gratified by keeping the children of #emph[Israel] in bondage, when God strove with him to bring him to let the people go. When God’s hand pressed #emph[Pharaoh] hard, and he was troubled with fears of God’s future wrath, he had some thoughts of letting the people go, and promised he would do it. But time after time he broke his promises when he saw there was a respite. When God filled #emph[Egypt] with thunder and lightning, and the fire ran along the ground, then #emph[Pharaoh] is brought to confess his sin with seeming humility, and to make a great resolution to let the people go. Exod 9:27, 28: “Then #emph[Pharaoh] summoned #emph[Moses] and #emph[Aaron.] ‘This time I have sinned,’ he said. ‘The #smallcaps[Lord] is righteous, and I and my people are wicked. Pray to the #smallcaps[Lord], for there has been enough of God’s thunder and hail. I will let you#super[+] go; you#super[+] do not need to stay any longer.’ ” So sinners are sometimes brought by thunders and lightnings, and great terrors of the law, to a seeming work of humiliation, and to all appearance to part with their sins. But they are no more thoroughly brought to a disposition to let them go than #emph[Pharaoh] was to let the people go. #emph[Pharaoh,] in the struggle between his conscience and his lusts, was for contriving a way in which God might be served and he might also enjoy his lusts, which were gratified by the slavery of the people. #emph[Moses] insisted that #emph[Israel]’s God should be served and sacrificed to. #emph[Pharaoh] was willing to consent to that, but would have it done without parting with the people. “Go, sacrifice to your#super[+] God within this land,” he says (Exod 8:25). So many sinners are for contriving to serve God and enjoy their lusts too. #emph[Moses] objected to complying with #emph[Pharaoh]’s proposal that serving God and yet staying in #emph[Egypt] under their taskmasters did not agree together, and were inconsistent with each other. (There is no serving God and continuing as slaves to such enemies of God at the same time.) After this #emph[Pharaoh] consented to let the people go, provided they would not go very far away. He was not willing to part with them finally, and so would have them within reach. So do many hypocrites with their sins. Afterward #emph[Pharaoh] consented to let the #emph[men] go, if they would leave the #emph[women] and #emph[children] (Exod 10:8, 9, 10). And then after that, when God’s hand was still harder on him, he consented that they should go, #emph[women] and #emph[children] as well as #emph[men,] provided they would leave their #emph[cattle] behind. But he was not willing to let them go with all they had (Exod 10:24). So it often is with sinners. They are willing to part with some of their sins, but not all. They are brought to part with the grosser acts of sin, but not to part with their lusts in lesser indulgences of them. Yet we must part with all our sins, little and great, and all that belongs to them, #emph[men, women, children] and #emph[cattle.] They must all be let go, #emph[with their young and their old, with their sons and their daughters, with their flocks and their herds; not a hoof must be left behind,] as #emph[Moses] told #emph[Pharaoh] concerning the children of #emph[Israel.] At last, when it came to the extreme, #emph[Pharaoh] consented to let the people all go, with all they had. But he was not steadfastly of that mind. He soon repented, and pursued them again. And the reason was that those lusts of pride and covetousness that were gratified by #emph[Pharaoh]’s dominion over the people, and the gains of their service, were never really mortified in him, but only forcibly restrained. And so, being guilty of backsliding after his seeming compliance with God’s commands, he was destroyed without remedy. In the same way there may be a forced parting with ways of disobedience to the commands of God that may seem to be universal, as far as appears, for a little while. But because it is nothing but force, without the mortification of the inward principle of sin, they will not persevere in it, but will return like the dog to its vomit, and so bring on themselves dreadful destruction without remedy. There were many false disciples in Christ’s time who followed him for a while. But none of them followed him to the end; some on one occasion and some on another went back and walked with him no more. #footnote[The counterfeit and common grace of foolish virgins, after some time of glorious profession, will certainly go out and be quite spent. It is consumed in the using and shining and burning.—Men who have been most forward decay: their gifts decay, their life decays.——It is so after some time of profession; for at first it grows rather than decays and withers, but afterward they have had enough of it, and it withers and dies.—The Spirit of God comes on many hypocrites in an abundant and plentiful measure of awakening grace. It comes on them as it did on #emph[Balaam,] and as it is with overflowing waters, which spread far, and grow very deep, and fill many empty places.—Though it does come on them so, yet it never rests within so as to dwell there, to take up an eternal mansion for himself.—That is why it decays little by little until at last it is quite gone, like ponds filled with rainwater that comes upon them, not spring water that rises up within them: it dries up little by little until it is quite dry. #emph[Shepard]’s parable, Part II. P. 58, 59. Some men may lay hold of Christ neither out of fear of misery nor only to preserve some sin. Rather, God lets in the light and heat of the blessed beams of the glorious gospel of the Son of God, showing that there is mercy, rich, free and sweet, for damned, great, vile sinners. “Good Lord,” says the soul, “what a sweet ministry, word, God and gospel this is!” And there it rests. This was the frame of the stony ground, which heard the word and received it with joy, and for a time believed. And this is the case of thousands who are much moved by the promise and mercy of Christ, and hang on free grace for a time. But like sweet smells in a room they do not last long; or like flowers they grow old and withered, and then fall. In time of temptation, lust and the world and sloth are sweeter than Christ and all his gospel. #emph[Shepard]’s parable, Part II. P. 168. There was never any carnal heart in whose soil some root of bitterness did not grow up at last. #emph[Shepard]’s parable, Part I. P. 195. We will see it in experience: take the best professing Christians living; though they may come to the Lord, as they and others judged, and follow the Lord, yet in time they will depart.—The Spirit was never given effectually to draw them, nor yet to keep them. #emph[Shepard]’s parable, Part I. P. 205.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[From what has been said it is clear that Christian practice, or a holy life, is #emph[a great and distinguishing sign] of true and saving grace. But I may go further and assert that it is #emph[the chief] of all the signs of grace, both as an evidence of the sincerity of professing Christians to others and also to their own consciences.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But then it is necessary that this be taken rightly, and that it be well understood and observed in what sense and manner Christian practice is the #emph[greatest sign] of grace. So, to set this matter in a clear light, I will try to prove particularly and distinctly that Christian practice is the #emph[principal sign] by which Christians are to judge of the sincerity of godliness, both their own and others’. At the same time I will observe some things that need to be particularly noted for a right understanding of this matter.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. I will consider Christian practice and a holy life as a manifestation and sign of the sincerity of a professing Christian #emph[to the eye of his neighbors] and brothers.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And that this is the #emph[chief sign] of grace in this respect is very evident from the word of God. Christ, who knew best how to give us rules for judging others, has repeated and pressed home that we should know them by their fruits. Matt 7:16: “By their fruit you#super[+] will recognize them.” And then, after arguing the point and giving clear reasons in the following verses why men’s fruits must be the chief evidence of what sort they are, he closes by repeating the assertion in verse 20: “So then, by their fruit you#super[+] will recognize them.” Again, ch. 12:33: “Make a tree good and its fruit will be good, or make a tree bad and its fruit will be bad.”—As much as to say: it is a very absurd thing for anyone to suppose that the tree is good and yet the fruit bad, that the tree is of one sort and the fruit of another; for the proper evidence of the nature of the tree is its fruit. Nothing else can be meant by the last clause of the verse, “for a tree is known by its fruit,” than that the tree is chiefly known by its fruit: that this is the main and most proper diagnostic by which one tree is distinguished from another. So Luke 6:44: “Each tree is known by its own fruit.” Christ nowhere says, “You#super[+] will know the tree by its leaves or flowers,” or “You#super[+] will know men by their talk,” or “You#super[+] will know them by the good story they tell of their experiences,” or “You#super[+] will know them by the manner and air of their speaking, and the emphasis and pathos of their expression,” or “by their speaking feelingly,” or “by their making a very great show by an abundance of talk,” or “by many tears and affectionate expressions,” or “by the affections you#super[+] feel in your#super[+] hearts toward them.” He says, #emph[by their fruit you#super[+] will recognize them; a tree is known by its fruit; each tree is known by its own fruit.] And as this is the evidence Christ has directed us mainly to look at in others when we judge them, so it is the evidence Christ has mainly directed us to give to others, by which they may judge of us. Matt 5:16: “Let your#super[+] light shine before men, that they may see your#super[+] good works and glorify your#super[+] Father in heaven.” Here Christ directs us to show our godliness to others. Godliness is, as it were, a light that shines in the soul. Christ directs that this light should not only shine within, but should #emph[shine] out #emph[before men,] so that they may see it. But in what way will this be? It is by our good works. Christ does not say, “that others, hearing your#super[+] good words, your#super[+] good story, or your#super[+] moving expressions,” but #emph[“that they may see your#super[+] good works and glorify your#super[+] Father in heaven.”] Doubtless when Christ gives us a rule for making our light shine, so that others may have evidence of it, his rule is the best there is. And the apostles mention Christian practice as the principal ground of their esteem of persons as true Christians. So the apostle #emph[Paul] does in the 6th chapter of #emph[Hebrews.] At the beginning of the chapter the apostle speaks there of those who have great common illuminations, who have #emph[been enlightened, have tasted the heavenly gift, have shared in the Holy Spirit, and have tasted the goodness of the word of God and the powers of the coming age, and afterward fall away,] and are like barren ground, whose #emph[curse is imminent; in the end it will be burned.] And then he immediately adds in the 9th verse (expressing his charitable judgment of the Christian #emph[Hebrews,] as having the saving grace that is better than all these common illuminations), “Even though we speak like this, beloved, we are convinced of better things in your#super[+] case—things that accompany salvation.” And then in the next verse he tells them why he had such good thoughts of them. He does not say that it was because they had given him a good account of a work of God on their souls and talked very experientially. It was their work and labor of love: “For God is not unjust. He will not forget your#super[+] work and labor of love that you#super[+] have shown for His name as you#super[+] have ministered to the saints and continue to do so.” And the same apostle speaks of faithfully serving God in practice as the proper proof to others of men’s loving Christ above all and preferring his honor to their private interest. Phil 2:21, 22: “For all the others look after their own interests, not those of Jesus Christ. But you#super[+] know the proof of him, that as a child with his father he has served with me to advance the gospel.” So the apostle #emph[John] expresses the same thing as the ground of his good opinion of #emph[Gaius.] 3 John 3, 4, 5, 6: “For I was overjoyed when the brothers came and testified of the truth that is in you.” But how did the brothers testify of the truth that was in #emph[Gaius?] And how did the apostle judge of the truth that was in him? It was not because they testified that he had given them a good account of the steps of his experiences, and talked like one who felt what he said, and had the very language of a Christian. They testified #emph[that he walked in the truth,] as it follows: #emph[“just as you continue to walk in the truth. I have no greater joy than to hear that my children are walking in the truth. Beloved, you are faithful in what you are doing for the brothers, and especially since they are strangers to you. They have testified to the church about your love.”] In this way the apostle explains what the brothers had borne witness to when they #emph[came and testified of his walking in the truth.] And in this same place the apostle seems to give it to #emph[Gaius] as a rule for how he should judge others. In verse 10 he mentions one #emph[Diotrephes,] who did not conduct himself well and led others away after him. Then in the 11th verse he directs #emph[Gaius] to beware of such men and not to follow them, and gives him a rule by which he may know them, exactly agreeing with the rule Christ had given before, #emph[By their fruit you#super[+] will recognize them.] The apostle says, “Beloved, do not imitate what is evil, but what is good. The one who does good is of God; the one who does evil has not seen God.” And I would further observe that the apostle #emph[James,] expressly comparing the way of showing others our faith and Christianity by our practice or works with other ways of showing our faith without works, or not by works, plainly and abundantly prefers the former. Jas 2:18: “But someone will say, ‘You have faith and I have works.’ Show me your faith without works, and I will show you my faith by my works.” A manifestation of our faith #emph[without works,] or in a way different from works, is a manifestation of it in #emph[words,] by which a man professes faith. As the apostle says in v. 14: “What good is it, my brothers, if someone #emph[says] he has faith?”—So here are two ways of showing our neighbor what is in our hearts: one by what we #emph[say,] and the other by what we #emph[do.] But the apostle abundantly prefers the latter as the best evidence. Now certainly all the accounts we give of ourselves in words (our saying that we have faith and that we are converted, and telling the manner in which we came to have faith, and the steps by which it was worked, and the discoveries and experiences that accompanied it) are still only showing our faith by what we #emph[say.] It is only showing our faith by our #emph[words,] which the apostle speaks of as falling vastly short of showing it by what we do, and showing our faith by our #emph[works.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And as Scripture plainly teaches that practice is the best evidence of the sincerity of professing Christians, so reason teaches the same thing. Reason shows that men’s deeds are better and more faithful interpreters of their minds than their words. The common sense of all mankind, through all ages and nations, teaches them to judge men’s hearts chiefly by their practice in other matters: whether a man is a loyal subject, a true lover, a dutiful child or a faithful servant. If a man professes a great deal of love and friendship to another, reason teaches all men that such a profession is not so great an evidence of his being a real and hearty friend as his showing himself a friend in deeds: being faithful and constant to his friend in prosperity and adversity, ready to spend himself, and deny himself, and suffer in his personal interest, to do him a kindness. A wise man will trust such evidences of the sincerity of friendship further than a thousand earnest professions and solemn declarations and most affectionate expressions of friendship in words. And there is equal reason why practice should also be looked on as the best evidence of friendship toward Christ. Reason says the same as Christ said in John 14:21: “Whoever has My commandments and keeps them is the one who loves Me.” So if we see a man who in the course of his life seems to follow and imitate Christ, and greatly to exert and deny himself for the honor of Christ and to promote his kingdom and interest in the world, reason teaches that this is an evidence of love to Christ more to be depended on than if a man only says he has love to Christ and tells of the inward experiences he has had of love to him, what strong love he felt, and how his heart was drawn out in love at such and such a time, when perhaps little imitation of Christ appears in his behavior. He seems reluctant to do any great thing for him, or to put himself out of his way to promote his kingdom, but seems inclined to excuse himself whenever he is called to deny himself for Christ. So it is if a man, in declaring his experiences, tells how he found his heart weaned from the world and saw its vanity, so that it all looked like nothing to him at such and such times, and professes that he gives up all to God, and calls heaven and earth to witness it; but yet in his practice he is violent in pursuing the world, and what he gets he keeps tight hold of. He is exceedingly unwilling to part with much of it for charitable and pious uses; it comes from him almost like his heart’s blood. But there is another professing Christian who does not say a great deal, yet in his behavior appears ready at all times to forsake the world whenever it stands in the way of his duty, and is free to part with it at any time to promote religion and the good of his fellow creatures. Reason teaches that the latter gives by far the more credible manifestation of a heart weaned from the world. And if a man appears to walk humbly before God and men, and to be of a conduct that savors of a broken heart, appearing patient and resigned to God under affliction and meek in his behavior among men, this is a better evidence of humiliation than if a person only tells how great a sense he had of his own unworthiness, how he was brought to lie in the dust, and was quite emptied of himself, and saw himself as nothing and as altogether filthy and abominable, #emph[etc.,] but yet acts as if he looked on himself as one of the first and best of saints, and by just right the head of all the Christians in the town, and is presumptuous, self-willed and impatient of the least contradiction or opposition. We may be sure in such a case that a man’s practice comes from a lower place in his heart than his profession. So (to mention no more instances) if a professing Christian shows in his behavior a pitying, tender spirit toward others in calamity, ready to bear their burdens with them, willing to spend his substance for them, and to suffer many inconveniences in his worldly interest to promote the good of others’ souls and bodies: is not this a more credible manifestation of a spirit of love to men than a man’s merely telling what love he felt for others at certain times, how he pitied their souls, how his soul was in labor for them, and how he felt a hearty love and pity for his enemies, when in his behavior he seems to be of a very selfish spirit, tight-fisted and stingy, all for himself and nothing for his neighbors, and perhaps envious and quarrelsome? Persons in a sudden surge of affection may think they have a willing heart for great things, to do much and to suffer much, and so may profess it very earnestly and confidently, when really their hearts are far from it. In this way many, in their surges of affection, have thought themselves willing to be damned eternally for the glory of God. Passing affections easily produce words, and words are cheap; and godliness is more easily feigned in words than in actions. Christian practice is a costly, laborious thing. The self-denial required of Christians, and the narrowness of the way that leads to life, do not consist in words but in practice. Hypocrites may much more easily be brought to talk like saints than to act like saints.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[So it is plain that Christian practice is the best sign or manifestation of the true godliness of a professing Christian to the eye of his neighbors.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But then the following things should be well observed, so that this matter may be rightly understood.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[First,] it must be observed that when Scripture speaks of Christian practice as the best evidence to others of sincerity and truth of grace, #emph[a profession of Christianity] is not excluded but presupposed. The rules mentioned were given to the followers of Christ to guide them in their thoughts of #emph[professing Christians,] and of those who offered themselves as members of their society, so that they might judge by them of the truth of their #emph[claims] and the sincerity of the #emph[profession] they made. They were not given for the trial of heathens, or of those who made no claim to Christianity and with whom Christians had nothing to do. This is as plain as possible in the great rule Christ gives in the 7th chapter of Matthew, #emph[By their fruit you#super[+] will recognize them.] He gives a rule there for judging those who professed to be Christians, indeed who made a very high profession: #emph[false prophets, who come in sheep’s clothing,] as in verse 15. So it is also with the words of the apostle #emph[James,] ch. 2:18: “Show me your faith without works, and I will show you my faith by my works.” It is evident that both these sorts of persons, offering to give these different evidences of their faith, are professors of faith. This is implied in each of them offering to give evidences of the faith he professed. And it is evident from the preceding verses that the apostle is speaking of those who profess faith in Jesus Christ. So it is very plain that the apostle #emph[John,] in the passages observed in his third epistle, is speaking of professing Christians. In these rules the Christian practice of those who profess is spoken of as the greatest and most distinguishing sign of their sincerity in their profession, much more of an evidence than their profession itself. Yet a profession of Christianity is plainly presupposed. It is not the main thing in the evidence, nor anything distinguishing in it; yet it is a thing required and necessary in it. In the same way, having an animal body is nothing that distinguishes a man from other creatures, and is not the main thing in the evidence of human nature; yet it is a thing required and necessary in the evidence. So if any man should say plainly that he was not a Christian, and did not believe that Jesus was the Son of God, or a person sent by God, these rules of Christ and his apostles do not at all oblige us to look on him as a sincere Christian, whatever his visible practice and virtues may be. And these rules have no place not only with respect to a man who explicitly denies Christianity and is a professed deist, Jew, heathen or open unbeliever, but also with respect to a man who merely refrains from making a profession of Christianity. For these rules were given us only for judging professing Christians. Fruits must be joined with open flowers; bells and pomegranates go together.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But here this question will naturally arise: when may a man be said to profess Christianity, or what profession may properly be called a profession of Christianity?]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[I answer in two things.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. For a man to be properly said to make a profession of Christianity, there must undoubtedly be a profession of all that is necessary to his being a Christian, or of as much as belongs to the essence of Christianity. Whatever is essential in Christianity itself, the profession of that is essential in the profession of Christianity. The profession must be of the thing professed. For a man to profess Christianity is for him to declare that he has it. And so, as much as belongs to a thing, so as to be necessary for its being truly called #emph[that] thing, so much is essential to the declaration of #emph[that] thing, for it to be truly called a declaration of #emph[that] thing. If we take only a part of Christianity, and leave out a part that is essential to it, what we take is not Christianity, because something of its essence is missing. So if we profess only a part, and leave out a part that is essential, what we profess is not Christianity. So, to make a profession of Christianity, we must profess that we believe that #emph[Jesus] is the #emph[Messiah,] for this reason: such a belief is essential to Christianity. And so we must profess, either expressly or implicitly, that #emph[Jesus] made satisfaction for our sins, and other essential doctrines of the gospel, because belief of these things is also essential to Christianity. But there are other things as essential to religion as an orthodox belief, and so it is just as necessary that we profess them if we are truly to be said to profess Christianity. So it is essential to Christianity that we repent of our sins; that we are convinced of our own sinfulness and have a sense that we have justly exposed ourselves to God’s wrath; that our hearts renounce all sin; that we embrace Christ with our whole hearts as our only Savior; that we love him above all and are willing for his sake to forsake all; and that we give ourselves up to be entirely and forever his, #emph[etc.] Such things as these belong as much to the essence of Christianity as the belief of any of the doctrines of the gospel, and so the profession of them belongs as much to a Christian profession. Not that, to be professing Christians, it is necessary to make an explicit profession of every single thing that belongs to Christian grace or virtue. But certainly there must be a profession, either express or implicit, of what is of the essence of religion. And as to the things Christians should #emph[express] in their profession, we ought to be guided by the precepts of God’s word, or by the Scripture examples of the public professions of religion God’s people have made from time to time. So they ought to profess their repentance of sin, as of old, when persons were admitted as professors, they came confessing their sins and showing their humiliation for sin (Matt 3:6). And the baptism they were baptized with was called #emph[the baptism of repentance] (Mark 1:4). And #emph[John,] when he had baptized them, urged them #emph[to bring forth fruit in keeping with repentance] (Matt 3:8), that is, agreeing with the repentance they had professed. He encouraged them that if they did so, they would escape the wrath to come and be gathered like wheat into God’s barn (Matt 3:7, 8, 9, 10, 12). So the apostle #emph[Peter] says to the #emph[Jews] in Acts 2:38, “Repent and be baptized,” which shows that repentance is a qualification that must be visible for baptism, and so ought to be publicly professed. So when the #emph[Jews] who returned from captivity entered publicly into covenant, it was with confession, or a public profession of repentance of their sins (Neh 9:2). This profession of repentance should include or imply a profession of conviction that God would be just in our damnation: see Neh 9:33, together with verse 35, and the beginning of the next chapter. They should profess their faith in Jesus Christ: that they embrace Christ and rely on him as their Savior with their whole hearts, and that they joyfully welcome the gospel of Christ. So #emph[Philip,] before baptizing the eunuch, required that he should profess that he #emph[believed with all his heart.] And those who were received as visible Christians at the great outpouring of the Spirit that began on the day of #emph[Pentecost] appeared #emph[gladly to receive the gospel.] Acts 2:41: “Those who gladly received his message were baptized, and about three thousand were added to the believers that day.” They should profess that they rely only on Christ’s righteousness and strength, that they are devoted to him as their only Lord and Savior, and that they rejoice in him as their only righteousness and portion. It is foretold that all nations would be brought to make this profession publicly (Isa 45:22 to the end): “Turn to Me and be saved, all the ends of the earth; for I am God, and there is no other. By Myself I have sworn; truth has gone out from My mouth, a word that will not be revoked: Every knee will bow before Me, every tongue will swear allegiance. Surely they will say of Me, ‘In the #smallcaps[Lord] alone are righteousness and strength.’ All who rage against Him will come to Him and be put to shame. In the #smallcaps[Lord] all descendants of #emph[Israel] will be justified and will exult.” They should profess to give themselves up entirely to Christ, and to God through him, as the children of #emph[Israel] did when they publicly acknowledged their covenant with God. Deut 26:17: “Today you have proclaimed that the #smallcaps[Lord] is your God and that you will walk in His ways, keep His statutes and commandments and ordinances, and listen to His voice.” They ought to profess a willing heart to embrace religion with all its difficulties, and to walk in a way of obedience to God universally and perseveringly (Exod 19:8, and 24:3, 7; Deut 26:16, 17, 18; 2 Kgs 23:3; Neh 10:28, 29; Ps 119:57, 106). They ought to profess that all their hearts and souls are in these commitments to be the Lord’s and to serve him forever (2 Chr 15:12, 13, 14). God’s people’s swearing to God, and swearing #emph[by] his name, or #emph[to] his name, as it might be translated (which seems to signify their solemnly giving themselves up to him in covenant, and vowing to receive him as their God and to be entirely his, to obey and serve him), is spoken of as a duty to be performed by all God’s visible #emph[Israel] (Deut 6:13, and 10:20; Ps 63:11; Isa 19:18; chapter 45:23, 24, compared with Romans 14:11 and Phil 2:10, 11; Isa 48:1, 2, and 65:15, 16; Jer 4:2, and 5:7, and 12:16; Hos 4:15, and 10:4). So, for persons to be entitled to full esteem and charity from their neighbors as sincere professors of Christianity, by the rules of Christ and his apostles mentioned before, there must be a visibly holy life, together with a profession either expressing or plainly implying such things as have now been mentioned. We are to #emph[know them by their fruits;] that is, we are to know by their fruits whether they are what they profess to be. We are not to know by their fruits that they have something in them which they do not so much as claim to have.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And moreover,]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. The profession of these things that is properly called a Christian profession, and that must be joined with Christian practice for persons to be entitled to the benefit of those rules, must be made with understanding (as far as appears). That is, they must be persons who appear to have been instructed in the principles of religion far enough to be in an ordinary capacity to understand the proper meaning of what is expressed in their profession. For sounds signify or declare nothing any further than men understand the meaning of their own sounds.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But for persons to make a proper profession of Christianity, such as Scripture directs, and such as the followers of Christ should require before accepting those who profess with full charity as members of their society, it is not necessary that they give an account of the particular steps and method by which the Holy Spirit, perceptibly to them, worked and brought about those great essential things of Christianity in their hearts. There is no trace in Scripture of any such practice of the apostles, or of the first ministers and Christians, requiring any such account before they received and treated others as their Christian brothers in every respect, or of their first examining them about the particular method and order of their experiences. They required of them a profession of the things worked, but no account of the manner of working was required of them. Nor is there the least shadow in Scripture of any such custom in the church of God from #emph[Adam] to the death of the apostle #emph[John.]]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[I am far from saying that it is not required that persons give any sort of account of their experiences to their brothers. For persons to profess the things in which the essence of Christianity lies is the same thing as to profess that they #emph[experience] those things. So it is when persons solemnly profess the following: that in a sense and full conviction of their own utter sinfulness, misery and helplessness, and totally undone state in themselves, and of their just desert of God’s utter rejection and eternal wrath without mercy, and of the utter insufficiency of their own righteousness, or of anything in them, to satisfy divine justice or recommend them to God’s favor, they depend only and entirely on the Lord Jesus Christ and his satisfaction and righteousness; that they believe the truth of the gospel of Christ with all their hearts; that in a full conviction and sense of his sufficiency and perfect excellence as a Savior, as set forth in the gospel, they cling to him with their whole souls and rest in him as the refuge and rest of their souls and the fountain of their comfort; that they repent of their sins, utterly renounce all sin, and give themselves up wholly to Christ, willingly submitting to him as their King; that they give him their hearts and their whole selves; that they are willing and resolved to have God for their whole and everlasting portion, and, depending on his promises of a future eternal enjoyment of him in heaven, to renounce all the enjoyments of this vain world, selling all for this great treasure and future inheritance, and to comply with every command of God, even the most difficult and self-denying, and to devote their whole lives to God’s service; and that, in forgiveness of those who have injured them and a general benevolence to mankind, their hearts are united to the people of Jesus Christ as their people, to cling to them and love them as their brothers, and to worship and serve God and follow Christ in union and fellowship with them, being willing and resolved to perform all the duties that belong to them as members of the same family of God and mystical body of Christ. I say, for persons solemnly to profess such things as these, as in the presence of God, is the same thing as to profess that they are conscious of, or #emph[experience,] such things in their hearts.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Nor do I suppose that persons’ giving an account of their experience of particular exercises of grace, with the times and circumstances, gives others no help in forming a judgment of their state; or that persons may not properly be asked about these in some cases, especially cases of great importance, where all possible satisfaction about persons’ piety is especially to be desired and sought, as in the ordination or approval of a minister. It may help in forming a judgment in several respects; among others in this, that by it we may be better satisfied that the one who professes speaks honestly and with understanding in what he professes, and that he does not make the profession as a mere formality. For a profession of Christianity to be accepted to any purpose, there ought to be good reason, from the circumstances of the profession, to think that the one who professes does not make it out of mere customary compliance with a prescribed form, using words without any distinct meaning, or in a very loose and ambiguous way, as confessions of faith are often subscribed; but that he signifies with understanding and honesty what he is conscious of in his own heart. Otherwise his profession can be of no significance, and is no more to be regarded than the sound of lifeless things. But in fact (whatever help an account of particular exercises may give in judging this) it must be granted that the best evidence of it is that the one who professes has first been thoroughly instructed by his teachers, and gives good proof of his sufficient knowledge, together with a practice agreeing with his profession.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Nor do I deny that when a person who is asked about particular passages, times and circumstances of his Christian experience seems, among other things, to be able to give a distinct account of the manner of his first conversion, in a way that has often been observed in true conversion, so that things seem perceptibly and distinctly to follow one another in the order of time according to the order of nature, this is an illustrating circumstance that, among other things, adds luster to the evidence he gives his brothers of the truth of his experiences.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But what I speak of as unscriptural is insisting on a particular account of the distinct method and steps in which the Spirit of God perceptibly proceeded in first bringing the soul into a state of salvation, as a thing required before receiving one who professes into full charity as a real Christian; or insisting on it so much that, for lack of such an account, other things in the evidence persons give their neighbors of their Christianity, things vastly more important and essential, are disregarded.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Secondly,] to understand rightly how Christian practice is the greatest evidence others can have of the sincerity of a professing Christian, we need to bear in mind what was said before in showing what Christian practice is, and to consider how far this may be visible to others. That a professing Christian is merely what is commonly called an honest man and a moral man (that is, we have no particular transgression or iniquity to charge him with that might bring a blot on his character) is no great evidence of the sincerity of his profession. This is not letting #emph[his light shine before men.] This is not that #emph[work and labor of love shown for Christ’s name] which gave the apostle such persuasion of the sincerity of the professing #emph[Hebrews] (Heb 6:9, 10). It may be that we see nothing in a man to show anything #emph[but that he may be] a good man. Nothing may appear in his life and conduct that is inconsistent with his being godly, and yet there may not be any great positive evidence #emph[that he is] so. But there may be great positive appearances of holiness in men’s visible behavior. Their life may appear to be a life of the service of God. They may appear to follow the example of Jesus Christ, and to come up in a great measure to the excellent rules in the 5th, 6th and 7th chapters of #emph[Matthew,] and the 12th of #emph[Romans,] and many other parts of the New Testament. There may be a great appearance of their being universal in their obedience to Christ’s commands and the rules of the gospel. They may appear to be universal in performing the duties of the first table, showing the fear and love of God; and also universal in fulfilling the rules of love to men, love to saints and love to enemies; rules of meekness and forgiveness; rules of mercy and charity, of looking not only to our own interests but also to the interests of others; rules of doing good to men’s souls and bodies, to particular persons and to the public; rules of temperance and mortification and of a humble conduct; rules of bridling the tongue, and of using it to glorify God and bless men, showing that the law of kindness is on their tongues. They may appear to walk as Christians in all places and at all seasons: in the house of God, in their families and among their neighbors, on Sabbath days and every day, in business and in conversation, toward friends and enemies, toward superiors, inferiors and equals. Persons in their visible walk may appear to be very earnestly engaged in the service of God and mankind, to labor and spend themselves much in this work of a Christian, and to be very constant and steadfast in it under all circumstances and temptations. There may be great evidences of a spirit willing to deny themselves and suffer for God and Christ, for the interest of religion and for the benefit of their brothers. There may be great appearances in a man’s walk of a disposition to forsake anything rather than forsake Christ, and to make everything give way to his honor. There may be great evidences in a man’s behavior that such religion as this is his element, and that he places the delight and happiness of his life in it. And his conduct may be such that he carries with him a sweet fragrance of Christian graces and heavenly dispositions wherever he goes. And when it is like this with those who profess Christianity, here is an evidence to others of their sincerity in their profession to which all other manifestations are not worthy to be compared.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[There is doubtless a great variety in the degrees of evidence professing Christians give of their sincerity in their life and practice, just as there is a variety in the fairness and clearness of the accounts persons give of the manner and method of their experiences. But undoubtedly such a manifestation of a Christian spirit in practice as has been described is vastly beyond the fairest and brightest story of particular steps and passages of experience that was ever told. And in general a manifestation of the sincerity of a Christian profession in practice is far better than an account of experiences.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But yet,]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Thirdly,] it must be noted, in agreement with what was observed earlier, that no outward manifestations and appearances whatever that are visible to the world are infallible evidences of grace. The manifestations that have been mentioned are the best mankind can have. They are such as oblige Christians to embrace wholeheartedly as saints those who profess, and to love them and rejoice in them as the children of God. They are enough to give them as great satisfaction concerning them as is ever needed to guide them in their conduct, or for any purpose that needs to be served in this world. But nothing that appears to them in their neighbor can be enough to produce absolute certainty about the state of his soul. For they do not see his heart; nor can they see all his outward behavior, for much of it is in secret and hidden from the eye of the world. And it is impossible to determine with certainty how far a man may go, from other principles, in many outward appearances and imitations of grace. Yet undoubtedly, if others could see as much of what belongs to men’s practice as their own consciences may see of it, it might be an infallible evidence of their state, as will appear from what follows.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Having considered Christian practice in this way as the #emph[best evidence] of the sincerity of professing Christians #emph[to others,] I now go on]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. to observe that Scripture also speaks of Christian practice as a distinguishing and sure evidence of grace to #emph[persons’ own consciences.] This is very plain in 1 John 2:3: “By this we can be sure that we have come to know Him: if we keep His commandments.” And the testimony of our consciences concerning our good deeds is spoken of as something that may give us assurance of our own godliness. 1 John 3:18, 19: “Little children, let us love not in word and speech, but in action” (in the original it is ΕΡΓΩ, #emph[in work]) “and truth. And by this we will know that we belong to the truth, and will assure our hearts in His presence.” And the apostle #emph[Paul] in Hebrews 6 speaks of #emph[the work and labor of love] of the Christian #emph[Hebrews] both as what persuaded him that they had something above the highest common illuminations, and as the evidence that tended to give them the highest assurance of hope concerning themselves. V. 9, etc.: “Even though we speak like this, beloved, we are convinced of better things in your#super[+] case—things that accompany salvation. For God is not unjust. He will not forget your#super[+] work and labor of love that you#super[+] have shown for His name as you#super[+] have ministered to the saints and continue to do so. We want each of you#super[+] to show this same diligence to the full assurance of hope to the very end.” So the apostle directs the #emph[Galatians] to examine their behavior or practice, so that they might have rejoicing in themselves in their own happy state. Gal 6:4: “Each one should test his own work. Then he will have reason to rejoice in himself alone, and not in someone else.” And the psalmist says in Ps 119:6, “Then I would not be ashamed when I consider all Your commandments”; that is, then I will be bold, assured and steadfast in my hope. And so it is with our Savior’s words in Matt 7:19, 20: “Every tree that does not bear good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire. So then, by their fruit you#super[+] will recognize them.” Though Christ gives this first as a rule by which we should judge others, yet in the words that follow next he plainly shows that he also intends it as a rule by which we should judge ourselves: “Not everyone who says to Me, ‘Lord, Lord,’ will enter the kingdom of heaven, but only he who #emph[does the will] of My Father in heaven. Many will say to Me on that day, ‘Lord, Lord,’ etc.—Then I will tell them plainly, ‘I never knew you#super[+]; depart from Me, #emph[you#super[+] workers of lawlessness!’] Therefore everyone who hears these words of Mine and #emph[acts on] them is like a wise man who built his house on the rock.—But everyone who hears these words of Mine and does not #emph[act on them] is like a foolish man who built his house on sand.” I will have occasion later to mention other texts that show the same thing.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But for greater clearness in this matter, I would #emph[first] show how Christian practice, doing good works or keeping Christ’s commandments, is to be taken when Scripture represents it as a sure sign to our own consciences that we are real Christians. And #emph[secondly,] I will prove that this is the chief of all the evidences men can have of their own sincere godliness.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[First,] I would show how Christian practice, or keeping Christ’s commandments, is to be taken when Scripture represents it as a sure evidence to our own consciences that we are sincere Christians.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And here I would observe that we cannot reasonably suppose that when Scripture speaks in this case of good works, good fruit and keeping Christ’s commandments, it has in view merely what is outward, the motion and action of the body, without including anything else, with no regard to any aim or intention of the agent, or to any act of his understanding or will. For consider men’s actions in that way, and they are no more good works or acts of obedience than the regular motions of a clock. Nor are they considered as the actions of the men, or as any human actions at all. The actions of the body, taken in that way, are neither acts of obedience nor of disobedience, any more than the motions of the body in a convulsion. But the obedience and fruit spoken of is the obedience and fruit of the man; and so not only the acts of the body, but the obedience of the soul, consisting in the acts and practice of the soul. Not that I suppose that when Scripture speaks in this case of gracious works and fruit and practice, these expressions include all inward piety and holiness of heart, both principle and exercise, both spirit and practice. For then, in giving these things as signs of a gracious principle in the heart, the same thing would be given as a sign of itself, and there would be no distinction between root and fruit. What is meant, and given as the sign of the holy principle and good state, is only the gracious exercise and holy act of the soul. Nor is every kind of inward exercise of grace meant, but the practical exercise: the exercise of the soul and exertion of inward holiness that there is in an act of obedience; or the exertion of the mind and act of grace that issues and ends in what are called the imperate acts of the will, the acts in which the soul directs and commands something to be done and brought about in practice.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Here, for a clearer understanding, I would observe that there are two kinds of exercises of grace. 1. There are what some call #emph[immanent] acts: that is, exercises of grace that remain within the soul, that begin and end there, without any immediate relation to anything to be done outwardly or brought about in practice. Such are the exercises of grace the saints often have in #emph[contemplation,] when the exercise in the heart does not proceed directly #emph[to,] or end #emph[in,] anything beyond the thoughts of the mind, however they may tend to practice more remotely (as all exercises of grace do). 2. There is another kind of acts of grace, which are more strictly called #emph[practical] or effective exercises, because they immediately concern something to be done. They are the exertions of grace in the commanding acts of the will, directing the outward actions. So it is when a saint gives a cup of cold water to a disciple in and from the exercise of the grace of charity, or willingly endures persecution in the way of his duty immediately from the exercise of a supreme love to Christ. Here is the exertion of grace producing its effect in outward actions. These exercises of grace are practical and productive of good works, not only in the sense that they are of a #emph[productive nature] (for so are all exercises of true grace), but they are the #emph[producing] acts. This is properly the exercise of grace in the act of the will, and this is properly the practice of the soul. And the soul is the immediate actor of no other practice but this. The motions of the body follow from the laws of union between the soul and body, which God, and not the soul, has fixed and maintains. The act of the soul, and the exercise of grace that is exerted in the performance of a good work, is the good work itself, as far as the soul is concerned in it, or as far as it is the soul’s good work. The determinations of the will are indeed our very actions, as far as they are properly ours, as Dr. #emph[Doddridge] observes. #footnote[Scripture doctrine of salvation. Sermon 1. P. 11.] This practice of the soul includes the aim and intention of the soul, which is the agent. For not only would we not look on the motions of a statue doing justice or giving alms by clockwork as any acts of obedience to Christ in that statue; but no one would call the voluntary actions of a man that outwardly and materially agree with a command of Christ by the name of obedience to Christ, if he had never heard of Christ or any of his commands, or had no thought of his commands in what he did.—If the acts of obedience and good fruits spoken of are looked on not as mere motions of the body but as acts of the soul, then the whole exercise of the spirit of the mind in the action must be taken in, with the end it acts for and the regard the soul then has to God. Otherwise they are no acts of self-denial, or obedience to God, or service done to him, but something else. Many of the martyrs have experienced such effective exercises of grace as I have now described in a high degree. And all true saints live a life of such acts of grace as these, since they all live a life of gracious works, of which these working exertions of grace are the life and soul. And this is the obedience and fruit God mainly looks at, since he looks at the soul more than the body, as much as the soul is the superior part in the make of human nature. As God looks at the obedience and practice of the man, he looks at the practice of the soul; for the soul is the man in God’s sight: “The #smallcaps[Lord] does not see as man does. … the #smallcaps[Lord] sees the heart.”]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And this is how obedience, good works and good fruit are to be taken when Scripture gives them as a sure evidence to our own consciences of a true principle of grace: as including the obedience and practice of the soul, which precedes and governs the actions of the body. When Scripture gives practice as the main evidence of our true Christianity to others, it means that part of our practice which is visible to them, our outward actions. But when practice is given as a sure evidence of our real Christianity to our own consciences, it means that part of our practice which is visible to our own consciences. This is not only the motion of our bodies, but the exertion and exercise of the soul that directs and commands that motion, which is more directly and immediately under the view of our own consciences than the act of the body. And that this is the meaning of Scripture is shown not only by the nature and reason of the thing; it is plain from Scripture itself. So it is evident that when Christ, at the conclusion of his Sermon on the Mount, speaks of doing or practicing these sayings of his as the grand sign that those who profess are true disciples (without it he likens them to a man who built his house on the sand, and with it to a man who built his house on a rock), he has in view not only the outward behavior but the inward exercise of the mind in that behavior. This is evident if we observe what those preceding sayings of his are that he refers to when he speaks of our doing or practicing them. We will find they are such as these: “Blessed are the poor in spirit,” “Blessed are those who mourn,” “Blessed are the meek,” “Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness,” “Blessed are the merciful,” “Blessed are the pure in heart,” “anyone who is angry with his brother,” etc., “anyone who looks at a woman to lust after her,” etc., “love your#super[+] enemies,” “do not worry about your#super[+] life,” and others of the same nature, which imply inward exercises. And when Christ says in John 14:21, “Whoever has My commandments and keeps them is the one who loves Me,” he evidently has special regard to the command repeated several times in the same discourse (which he calls, by way of eminence, #emph[his commandment]), #emph[that they should love one another as he had loved them] (see ch. 13:34, 35, and ch. 15:10, 12, 13, 14). But this command chiefly concerns an exercise of the mind or heart, though exerted in practice. So when the apostle #emph[John] says in 1 John 2:3, “By this we can be sure that we have come to know Him: if we keep His commandments,” he plainly has chief regard to the same command, as appears from what follows, verses 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, and 2nd Epistle, verses 5, 6. And when we are told in Scripture that men will be judged at the last day #emph[according to their works,] and that #emph[all will receive according to the things done in the body,] it is not to be understood only of outward acts. For if it were, why is God so often spoken of as searching the hearts and testing the inmost minds, #emph[so that he may give to every one according to his works?] As in Rev 2:23: “And all the churches will know that I am the One who searches minds and hearts, and I will repay each of you#super[+] according to your#super[+] works.” Jer 17:9, 10: “I, the #smallcaps[Lord], search the heart; I examine the mind to reward a man according to his way, according to the fruit of his deeds.” But if by #emph[his way and the fruit of his deeds] only the actions of his body are meant, what need is there of #emph[searching the heart and examining the mind] in order to know them? #emph[Hezekiah] in his sickness pleads his practice as an evidence of his title to God’s favor, including not only his outward actions but what was in his heart. Isa 38:3: “Please, O #smallcaps[Lord], remember how I have walked before You faithfully and with wholehearted devotion.”]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Though in this great evidence of sincerity that Scripture gives us what is inward is of the greatest importance, yet what is outward is included and intended, as connected with the practical exertion of grace in the will directing and commanding the actions of the body. And by this every claim any man can make to evidences of godliness while he lives wickedly outwardly is effectually cut off. For the great evidence lies in the inward exercise and practice of the soul that consists in the act of the will commanding outward acts. But it is well known that these commanding acts of the will do not go one way and the actions of the bodily organs another. For the unalterable law of nature is that they are united, as long as soul and body are united, and the organs are not so destroyed as to be incapable of the motions the soul commands. So it would be ridiculous for a man to plead that the commanding act of his will was to go to public worship while his feet carry him to a tavern or a brothel; or that the commanding act of his will was to give a coin he had in his hand to a poor beggar while his hand at the same instant kept it back and held it tight.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Secondly,] I go on to show that Christian practice, taken in the sense that has been explained, is #emph[the chief] of all the evidences of a saving sincerity in religion to the consciences of those who profess it. It is much to be preferred to the method of the first convictions, enlightenings and comforts in conversion, or to any immanent discoveries or exercises of grace whatever that begin and end in contemplation. #footnote[Look at #emph[John,] Christ’s beloved disciple and bosom companion. He had received the anointing to know him who is true, #emph[and he knew that he knew him] (1 John 2:3). But how did he know that? He might be deceived (for it is strange to see what a melancholy imagination will do, and its effects; as honest men are reputed to have weak brains, and never to have seen the depths of the secrets of God). What is his final proof? #emph[Because we keep his commandments.] #emph[Shepard]’s Par. Part I. P. 131. A man may know his present union with the Lord Jesus by a work. 1 John 2:4: “If anyone says, ‘I know Him,’ but does not keep His commandments, he is a liar.”—Yes, that is true negatively; but may a man, ought a man, to see or know his union positively by this? Answer: verse 5. Many said they did know and love the Lord, but “if anyone keeps His word”—Oh, they are sweet! It is heaven to cling to him in every command; it is death to depart from any command. “By this we know that we are in Him.” If it were possible to ask the angels how they know they are not devils, they would answer, “The Lord’s will is ours.” #emph[Shepard]’s Par. Part I. P. 134. If the question is, whom does the Lord Jesus love, you need not go to heaven for the answer; the word is near you: #emph[those who love Christ.] Who are those? #emph[Those who keep his commandments.] #emph[Shepard]’s Par. Part I. P. 138. Will you have Christ sit in heaven, and not look for him to subdue your lusts by the work of his grace, and so rule your hearts? Then you despise his kingdom. Do you seek pardon in the blood of Christ, and never look for the virtue and end of that blood, to wash you and make you without spot, #emph[etc.?] Then you despise his priesthood and blood. Do you look for Christ to do work for you, while you do not do Christ’s work and bring forth fruit to him? Then you despise his honor (John 15:8). If I were to point out a hypocrite, or a false heart, this is what I would say: it is he who will set up Christ but loathe his work. #emph[Shepard]’s parable Part I. P. 140.] The evidence of this appears from the following arguments.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Argument] I. Reason plainly shows that the things which put to the proof what men will actually hold to and prefer in their practice, when left to follow their own choice and inclinations, are the proper trial of what they really prefer in their hearts. Sincerity in religion, as has already been observed, consists in setting God highest in the heart, in choosing him before other things, in having a heart to sell all for Christ, #emph[etc.]— But a man’s actions are the proper trial of what his heart prefers. For instance, when God and other things come to stand in competition, when God is, as it were, set before a man on one hand and his worldly interest or pleasure on the other (as often happens in the course of a man’s life), his behavior in such a case, in actually holding to the one and forsaking the other, is the proper trial of which he prefers. Sincerity consists in forsaking all for Christ in heart. But to forsake all for Christ in heart is the very same thing as to have a heart to forsake all for Christ. And certainly the proper trial of whether a man has a heart to forsake all for Christ is his being actually put to it: Christ and other things coming into competition, so that he must actually or practically hold to one and forsake the other. To forsake all for Christ in heart is the same thing as to have a heart to forsake all for Christ when called to it. But the highest proof to ourselves and others that we have a heart to forsake all for Christ when called to it is actually doing it when called to it, or as far as we are called to it. To follow Christ in heart is to have a heart to follow him. To deny ourselves in heart for Christ is the same thing as to have a heart to deny ourselves for him in fact. The main and most proper proof of a man’s having a heart to anything about which he is free to follow his own inclinations, and either to do it or not as he pleases, is his doing it. When a man is free to speak or keep silent, the most proper evidence of his having a heart to speak is his speaking. When a man is free to walk or sit still, the proper proof of his having a heart to walk is his walking. Godliness does not consist in a heart to intend to do the will of God, but in a heart to do it. The children of #emph[Israel] in the wilderness had the former, as we read in Deut 5:27, 28, 29: “Go near and listen to all that the #smallcaps[Lord] our God says. Then you can tell us everything the #smallcaps[Lord] our God tells you; we will listen and obey.” “And the #smallcaps[Lord] heard the words you#super[+] spoke to me, and He said to me, ‘I have heard the words that these people have spoken to you. They have done well in all that they have spoken. If only they had such a #emph[heart] to fear Me and keep all My commandments always, so that it might be well with them and with their children forever.’ ” The people showed that they had #emph[a heart to intend to keep God’s commandments,] and to be very forward in those intentions. But God shows that this was far from being the thing he desired, in which true godliness consists: #emph[a heart actually to keep them.]]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[So it is exceedingly absurd, and even ridiculous, for anyone to claim that he has a good heart while he lives a wicked life, or does not bring forth the fruit of universal holiness in his practice. For it is proved in fact that such men do not love God above all. It is foolish to argue against plain fact and experience. Men who live in ways of sin, and yet flatter themselves that they will go to heaven, or expect to be received hereafter as holy persons without a holy life and practice, act as though they expected to make a fool of their judge. This is implied in what the apostle says (speaking of men’s doing good works and living a holy life, and so showing evidence of their title to everlasting life) in Gal 6:7: “Do not be deceived: God is not to be mocked. Whatever a man sows, he will reap in return.” As much as to say: Do not deceive yourselves with an expectation of reaping life everlasting hereafter if you do not sow to the Spirit here. It is useless to think that God will be made a fool of by you, that he will be shamed and tricked with shadows instead of substance, and with empty claims instead of the good fruit he expects, when the contrary of what you claim appears plainly in your life before his face. The word #emph[mock] is sometimes used in Scripture in this way. So #emph[Delilah] says to #emph[Samson, “You have mocked me and lied to me!”] (Judg 16:10, 13), that is, “You have tricked me, as though you would make a fool of me, as if I might easily be turned away with any empty pretense instead of the truth.” So it is said that when #emph[Lot] told his sons-in-law that God would destroy that place, #emph[he seemed to his sons-in-law like one who was mocking] (Gen 19:14), that is, he seemed like one who would make a game of them, as though they were such credulous fools as to pay attention to such bugbears. But the great judge, whose eyes are like a flame of fire, will not be mocked or tricked with any claims without a holy life. Men may have prophesied in his name, and worked miracles, and had faith so that they could move mountains, and cast out devils. However high their religious affections have been, however great the resemblances of grace they have had, and though their hiding place has been so dark and deep that no human skill or search could find them out, yet if they are #emph[workers] or #emph[practicers of iniquity,] they cannot hide their hypocrisy from their judge. Job 34:22: “There is no darkness or deep shadow where the #emph[workers of iniquity] can hide.” Would a wise prince allow himself to be fooled and tricked by a subject who claimed that he was a loyal subject? Suppose he told his prince that he had an entire affection for him, that at such and such a time he had experience of it and felt his affections working strongly toward him, and came expecting to be accepted and rewarded by his prince as one of his best friends on that account, though he lived in rebellion against him, following some pretender to his crown and from time to time stirring up sedition against him. Or would a master allow himself to be shamed and duped by a servant who claimed great experiences of love and honor toward him in his heart, and a great sense of his worthiness and kindness to him, when at the same time he refused to obey him, and he could get no service done by him?]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Argument] II. As reason shows that the things that occur in the course of life which put to the proof whether men will prefer God to other things in practice are the proper trial of the uprightness and sincerity of their hearts, so Scripture represents the same things as the proper trial of the sincerity of those who profess. There we find such things called by that very name, #emph[trials] or #emph[temptations] (which, as I observed before, are words of the same meaning). The things that put to the proof whether men will prefer God to other things in practice are the difficulties of religion, the things that occur which make the practice of duty difficult and contrary to other principles besides the love of God. For in them God and other things are both set before men together for their actual and practical choice, and it comes to this: we cannot hold to both, but one or the other must be forsaken. And all through Scripture these things are called by the name of trials or proofs. #footnote[2 Cor 8:2. Heb 11:36. 1 Pet 1:7. Chapter 4:12. Gen 22:1. Deut 8:2, 16. Chapter 13:3. Exod 15:25. And 16. 4. Judg 2:22. Chapter 3:1, 4. Ps 66:10, 11. Dan 12:10. Rev 3:10. Job 23:10. Zech 13:9. Jas 1:12. Rev 2:10. Luke 8:13. Acts 20:19. Jas 1:2, 3. 1 Pet 1:6.] And they are called by this name because by them those who profess are tried and proved, what sort they are, whether they are really what they profess and appear to be; and because in them the reality of a supreme love to God is brought to the test of experiment and fact. They are the proper proofs in which it is truly settled by experience whether or not men have a thorough disposition of heart to hold to God. Deut 8:2: “Remember that these forty years the #smallcaps[Lord] your God led you all the way in the wilderness, so that He might humble you and test you in order to know what was in your heart, whether or not you would keep His commandments.” Judg 2:21, 22: “I will no longer drive out before them any of the nations Joshua left when he died. In this way I will test whether #emph[Israel] will keep the #emph[way of the #smallcaps[Lord]].” So also ch. 3:1, 4, and Exod 16:4. And when Scripture calls these difficulties of religion by the name of temptations or trials, it explains itself to mean by this the trial or experiment of their faith. Jas 1:2, 3: “Consider it pure joy, my brothers, when you#super[+] encounter trials of many kinds, because you#super[+] know that the testing of your#super[+] faith develops perseverance.” 1 Pet 1:6, 7: “Though now for a little while you#super[+] may have had to suffer grief in various trials so that the trial of your#super[+] faith—more precious than gold,” etc. So the apostle #emph[Paul] speaks of the costly duty of parting with our substance to the poor as the proof of the sincerity of the love of Christians (2 Cor 8:8). And the difficulties of religion are often represented in Scripture as the trial of those who profess, in the same way that the furnace is the proper trial of gold and silver. Ps 66:10, 11: “For You, O God, have tested us; You have refined us like silver. You led us into the net; You laid burdens on our backs.” Zech 13:9: “This third I will bring through the fire; I will refine them like silver and test them like gold.” What has the color and appearance of gold is put into the furnace to try whether it is what it seems to be, real gold or not. So the difficulties of religion are called trials because they try those who have the profession and appearance of saints, whether they are what they appear to be, real saints. If we put true gold into the furnace, we will find its great value and preciousness. So the truth and priceless value of the virtues of a true Christian appear when they are under these trials. 1 Pet 1:7: “so that the trial of your#super[+] faith—more precious than gold, which perishes …—may result in praise, glory, and honor.” True and pure gold will come out of the furnace at full weight; so true saints, when tried, come forth as gold (Job 23:10). Christ distinguishes true grace from counterfeit by this, that it is #emph[gold refined by fire] (Rev 3:17, 18). So it is evident that these things are called #emph[trials] in Scripture chiefly because they try or prove the sincerity of those who profess. And from what has now been observed it is evident that they are the most proper trial or proof of their sincerity, since the very meaning of the word #emph[trial,] as it is ordinarily used in Scripture, is the difficulty occurring in the way of a professing Christian’s duty, as the trial or experiment of his sincerity. If #emph[trial of sincerity] is the proper name of these difficulties of religion, then doubtless these difficulties of religion are #emph[properly and eminently] the trial of sincerity. For doubtless they are eminently what the Holy Spirit calls them; God gives things their names from what is eminently their nature. And if these things are the proper and eminent trial, proof or experiment of the sincerity of those who profess, then certainly the result of the trial or experiment (that is, persons’ behavior or practice under such trials) is the proper and eminent evidence of their sincerity. For they are called trials or proofs only with regard to the result, and because the effect is eminently the #emph[proof] or #emph[evidence.] And this is the most proper proof and evidence to the consciences of those who undergo these trials. For when God is said by these things to #emph[try men and prove them, to see what is in their hearts, and whether or not they will keep his commandments,] we are not to understand that it is for his own information, or so that he may obtain evidence of their sincerity for himself (for he needs no trials for his information), but chiefly for their conviction, and to set evidence before their consciences. #footnote[I am persuaded, as #emph[Calvin] is, that all the various trials of men are meant to show them to themselves, and to the world, that they are only counterfeits, and to make saints better known to themselves.—Rom 5:4: #emph[Tribulation works trial, and trial hope.] Prov 17:3. If you want to know whether it will hold its weight, the trial will tell you. #emph[Shepard]’s parable Part I. P. 191.] So when God is said to prove #emph[Israel] by the difficulties they met with in the wilderness, and by the difficulties they met with from their enemies in #emph[Canaan,] to know what was in their hearts, whether or not they would keep his commandments, it must be understood that it was to show them to themselves, so that they might know what was in their own hearts. So when God tempted or tried #emph[Abraham] with the difficult command to offer up his son, it was not for God’s own satisfaction, whether or not #emph[Abraham] feared God, but for #emph[Abraham]’s own greater satisfaction and comfort, and the clearer manifestation of the favor of God to him. When #emph[Abraham] had proved faithful under this trial, God says to him, “Now I know that you fear God, since you have not withheld your only son from Me.” This plainly implies that in this practical exercise of #emph[Abraham]’s grace under this trial there was a clearer evidence of the truth of his grace than there ever was before, and the greatest evidence to #emph[Abraham]’s conscience. For God himself gives it to #emph[Abraham] as such, for his comfort and rejoicing, and speaks of it to him as what might be the greatest evidence to his conscience of his being upright in the sight of his judge. This proves what I say, that holy practice under trials is the highest evidence of the sincerity of those who profess to their own consciences. And we find that Christ again and again took the same method to convince the consciences of those who claimed friendship with him, and to show them what they were. This was the method he took with the rich young man (Matt 19:16, #emph[etc.]). He seemed to show great respect to Christ. He came kneeling to him, and called him #emph[good Teacher,] and made a great profession of obedience to the commandments. But Christ tried him by telling him to #emph[go and sell all that he had and give to the poor, and come, take up his cross and follow him,] telling him #emph[that then he would have treasure in heaven.] So he tried another we read of in Matthew 8:20. He made a great profession of respect to Christ. “Lord,” he says, “I will follow You wherever You go.” Christ immediately puts his friendship to the proof by telling him that #emph[foxes had dens and the birds of the air had nests, but the Son of Man had no place to lay his head.] And in this way Christ still usually tries professed disciples in general in his providence. So it is with the seed sown in every kind of ground, stony ground, thorny ground and good ground. Though in all of them it looks alike when it first springs up, yet it is tried, and the difference is made to appear, by the burning heat of the sun.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Since, then, these are the things God uses to try us, it is undoubtedly the surest way for us to pass a right judgment on ourselves to try ourselves by the same things. These trials of his are not for his information but for ours, so we ought to take our information from them. The surest way to know our gold is to look at it and examine it in God’s furnace, where he tries it for this very end, that we may see what it is. If we want to know whether or not a building stands strong, we must look at it when the wind blows. If we want to know whether what has the form of wheat has the real substance of wheat, or is only chaff, we must watch it when it is winnowed. If we want to know whether a staff is strong, or a rotten, broken reed, we must watch it when it is leaned on and weight is put on it. If we want to weigh ourselves justly, we must weigh ourselves in God’s scales, the ones he uses to weigh us. #footnote[Dr. #emph[Sibbs,] in his bruised reed, says: When Christ’s will comes into competition with any worldly loss or gain, if the heart will stoop to Christ then, in that particular case, it is a true sign. For the truest trial of the power of grace is in such particular cases as touch us most nearly, for there our corruption gathers the greatest strength. When Christ came home to the young man in the gospel, he lost a disciple in him. Mr. #emph[Flavel] speaks of a holy practice under trials as the greatest evidence of grace. “No man,” he says, “can say what he is, whether his graces are true or false, until they are tried and examined by the things that are to them as fire is to gold.” #emph[Touchstone of sincerity,] ch. 4. Sect. 1. Again, speaking of great difficulties and sufferings in the way of duty, in which a person must actually part either with what is dearest to him of a worldly nature or with his duty, he says: That such sufferings as these will show the falseness and rottenness of men’s hearts cannot be doubted, if you consider that this is the fire God designed for this very use and purpose, to separate the gold from the dross. So you will find it in 1 Pet 4:12: “Beloved, do not be surprised at the fiery trial that has come upon you#super[+] to try you#super[+]”; that is, the very design and aim of providence in permitting and ordering these sufferings is to try you. On this account you find the hour of persecution (in a fitting sense) called the hour of temptation or testing (Rev 3:10). For then those who profess are sifted to the very bran, searched to their very deepest principles. This is #emph[the day burning like a furnace, when every evildoer will be stubble] (Mal 4:1). For in that day the ruling interest must appear and be shown; it can be hidden no longer. “No servant can serve two masters,” says Christ (Luke 16:13). A man may serve many masters if they all command the same thing, or things subordinate to each other; but he cannot serve two masters if their commands clash and conflict with each other. And such are the commands of Christ and of the flesh in an hour of suffering:—so the two interests come into full opposition. And now only have patience and wait a little, and you will discern which is ruling. A dog follows two men while they both walk the same way, and you do not know which of the two is his master. Only wait a little, until their paths part, and then you will quickly see who is his master. So it is in this case. Ibid ch. 8. Sect. 3. And in another chapter he says: Great numbers of persons are deceived and destroyed by trusting to grace that seems real but is untried. This was the miserable condition of the #emph[Laodicean] professing Christians: they counted themselves rich, but were really poor. All that glitters is not gold. Their gold (as they counted it) was never tried in the fire. If a man’s whole estate lay in some precious stone, suppose a rich diamond, how concerned he is to have it thoroughly tried, to see whether it will bear a sharp blow with the hammer, or fly to pieces like a #emph[Bristol] diamond! Ibid ch. 10. Sect. 3. Again in the same place: The promises of salvation are made over to tried grace, and only to such grace as will endure the trial. The Lord will try you. God has his trying times, and they were never sent except to show who were dross and who were gold. And the main end of all God’s trials is to show this truth that I am now pressing on you. Some have a thorough work, and then the trial shows the truth, as in #emph[Abraham] (Heb 11:17). Some have a superficial work, and they fall in the trial, as in #emph[Saul;] and it shows that it was only a shallow work. For this is the question God puts: is it thorough or not? “Ay,” says the carnal heart; “yes,” says a gracious heart. That is why it is strange to see what men will do when a trial comes. #emph[Shepard]’s Par. Part I. P. 219. There is an hour of temptation that tries men, which will show what men really are. #emph[Shepard]’s parable Part II. P. 60.] These trials in the course of our practice are, as it were, the balances in which our hearts are weighed; or in which Christ and the world, or Christ and his rivals, are weighed as to the esteem and regard they have in our hearts, or are put into opposite scales, so that there is opportunity to see which outweighs the other. When a man is brought to the parting of paths, one of which leads to Christ and the other to the objects of his lusts, to see which way he will go; or is brought and, as it were, set between Christ and the world, Christ on the right hand and the world on the left, so that if he goes to one he must leave the other, to see which his heart inclines to most, or which outweighs the other in his heart: this is just the same thing as laying Christ and the world in two opposite scales. And his going to the one and leaving the other is just the same thing as the sinking of one scale and the rising of the other. So a man’s practice under the trials of God’s providence is as much the proper experiment and evidence of the superior inclination of his heart as the motion of the balance, with different weights in opposite scales, is the proper experiment of the superior weight.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Argument] III. Another argument that holy practice, in the sense explained, is the highest kind of evidence of the truth of grace to the consciences of Christians is this: in the language of Scripture, grace is said to be made perfect, or to be finished, in practice. So the apostle James says in Jas 2:22: “You see that his faith was working with his works, and his faith was perfected by his works,” or finished (as the word in the original properly means). So the love of God is said to be made perfect, or finished, in keeping his commandments. 1 John 2:4, 5: “If anyone says, ‘I know Him,’ but does not keep His commandments, he is a liar, and the truth is not in him. But if anyone keeps His word, the love of God has been truly perfected in him.” The commandment of Christ the apostle especially has in view when he speaks here of our keeping his commandments is (as I observed before) that great commandment of his which concerns deeds of love to our brothers, as appears from the following verses. Again, the love of God is said to be perfected in the same sense in ch. 4:12: “If we love one another, God remains in us, and His love is perfected in us.” Here doubtless the apostle still has in view loving one another in the way he had explained in the preceding chapter, where he speaks of loving one another as a sign of the love of God, verses 17, 18: “If anyone with earthly possessions … withholds his compassion from him,” etc., “how can the love of God abide in him? Little children, let us love not in word and speech, but in action” (or in work) “and truth.” By #emph[loving in work] in this way, the apostle says, #emph[the love of God is perfected in us.] Grace is said to be perfected or finished in holy practice because in it grace is brought to its proper effect, and to the exercise that is the end of the principle. In it the tendency and design of grace is reached, and its working completed and crowned. So the tree is made perfect in the fruit. It is not perfected in the seed’s being planted in the ground. It is not perfected in the first quickening of the seed and in its putting out root and shoot. Nor is it perfected when it comes up out of the ground, nor in bringing forth leaves, nor yet in putting forth blossoms. But when it has brought forth good ripe fruit, then it is perfected. In this it reaches its end; the design of the tree is finished; all that belongs to the tree is completed and brought to its proper effect in the fruit. So it is with grace in its practical exercises. Grace is said to be made perfect or finished in its work or fruit in the same way as is said of sin. Jas 1:15: “Then after lust has conceived, it gives birth to sin; and sin, when it is finished, gives birth to death.” Here are three steps. #emph[First,] sin in its principle or habit, in the being of lust in the heart. Next, here is its conceiving, consisting in its immanent exercises in the mind. And #emph[lastly,] here is the fruit that was conceived actually brought forth, in the wicked work and practice. And this the apostle calls the #emph[finishing] or #emph[perfecting] of sin, for the word in the original is the same as is translated #emph[perfected] in the places mentioned before.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Now certainly, if this is so, if grace is made perfect in its fruit in this way, if these practical exercises of grace are the exercises in which grace is brought to its proper effect and end, the exercises in which whatever belongs to its design, tendency and working is completed and crowned, then these exercises must be the highest evidences of grace, above all other exercises. Certainly the proper nature and tendency of every principle must appear best and most fully in its most perfect exercises, or in the exercises in which its nature is most completely exerted, and its tendency most fully answered and crowned in its proper effect and end. If we want to see the proper nature of anything whatever, and to see it fully distinguished from other things, let us look at it in its finishing. The apostle #emph[James] says, #emph[faith was perfected by works,] and brings this in as an argument to prove that works are the chief evidence of faith, by which the sincerity of those who profess faith is justified (Jas 2). And the apostle #emph[John,] after telling us again and again that love was made perfect in keeping Christ’s commandments, observes in 1 John 4:18 “that perfect love drives out fear,” meaning (at least in part) love made perfect in this sense. This agrees with what he had said in the preceding chapter, #emph[that by loving in deed, or work, we know that we belong to the truth, and will assure our hearts] (vv. 18, 19).]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Argument] IV. Another thing that makes it evident that holy practice is the principal evidence we ought to use in judging the sincerity both of ourselves and of others is that Scripture insists on this evidence above all others. A common acquaintance with Scripture, together with a little attention and observation, will be enough to show anyone that throughout Scripture, from the beginning of #emph[Genesis] to the end of #emph[Revelation,] this is insisted on as a mark of true piety ten times more than anything else. And in the New Testament, where Christ and his apostles expressly and with declared purpose lay down signs of true godliness, this is insisted on almost entirely. It may be observed that Christ and his apostles not only often say things in their discourses on the great doctrines of religion that show what the nature of true godliness must be, or from which its nature and signs may be inferred by just consequence, and often mention in passing many things that belong to godliness. They also often, on set purpose, give signs and marks for the trial of those who profess, urging them to try themselves by the signs they give, and introducing what they say with expressions like these: #emph[By this you#super[+] will know that you#super[+] know God; by this the children of God and the children of the devil are made plain; he who has this builds on a good foundation; he who does not have it builds on the sand; by this we will assure our hearts; he is the man who loves Christ,] etc. But I can find no place where either Christ or his apostles give signs of godliness in this way (and the places are many) in which Christian practice is not almost the only thing insisted on. It is true that in many of these places #emph[love to the brothers] is spoken of as a sign of godliness; and (as I have observed before) there is no one virtuous affection or disposition so often expressly spoken of as a sign of true grace as our having love for one another. But then the Scriptures explain themselves to mean chiefly this love as exercised and expressed in practice, or in deeds of love. So the apostle #emph[John] (who above all others insists on love to the brothers as a sign of godliness) most expressly explains himself in 1 John 3:14: “We know that we have passed from death to life, because we love our brothers. The one who does not love remains in death.—If anyone with earthly possessions sees his brother in need, but withholds his compassion from him, how can the love of God abide in him? Little children, let us love not in word and speech, but in action” (that is, in deeds of love) “and truth. And by this we will know that we belong to the truth, and will assure our hearts in His presence.” So when Scripture insists so much on our loving one another as a great sign of godliness, we are not to understand by it the immanent workings of affection that men feel toward one another so much as the soul’s practicing all the duties of the second table of the law, all of which, the New Testament tells us again and again, a true love for one another includes (Rom 13:8, and 10; Gal 5:14; Matt 22:39, 40). So in reality there is no place in the New Testament where the declared design is to give signs of godliness in which holy practice and keeping Christ’s commandments is not the mark chosen out of all others to be insisted on. This is an invincible argument that it is the chief of all the evidences of godliness, unless we suppose that when Christ and his apostles deliberately set about this business of giving signs by which professing Christians in all ages might determine their state, they did not know how to choose signs as well as we could have chosen them for them. But if we make the word of Christ our rule, then undoubtedly the marks Christ and his apostles chiefly laid down and gave us, so that we might try ourselves by them, are the same marks we ought especially to receive and chiefly to use in trying ourselves. #footnote[It is a sure rule (says Dr. #emph[Preston]) that what the Scriptures spend many words on, we should spend many thoughts on; and what the Holy Spirit urges most, we should prize most. #emph[Churches carriage.]] And surely the things Christ and his apostles chiefly insisted on in the rules they gave, ministers ought chiefly to insist on in the rules they give. To insist much on things Scripture insists little on, and to insist very little on things Scripture insists much on, is a dangerous thing. For it is going out of God’s way, and judging ourselves and guiding others in an unscriptural manner. God knew which way of leading and guiding souls was safest and best for them. He insisted so much on some things because he knew they needed to be insisted on; and, as a wise God, he left other things more alone because he knew it was not best for us to lay so much of the weight of the trial there. As the Sabbath was made for man, so the Scriptures were made for man, and infinite wisdom has fitted them for our use and benefit. So we should make them our guide in all things, in our thoughts of religion and of ourselves. And for us to make great what Scripture makes little, and little what Scripture makes great, tends to give us a monstrous idea of religion, and (at least indirectly and gradually) to lead us wholly away from the right rule and from a right opinion of ourselves, and to establish delusion and hypocrisy.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Argument] V. Christian practice #emph[is plainly spoken of] in the #emph[word of God] as the main evidence of the truth of grace, not only to others but to men’s own consciences. Not only is it spoken of and insisted on more than other signs, but in many places where it is spoken of it is represented as the chief of all evidences. This is plain from the manner of expression again and again. Suppose God were now to speak from heaven to settle our doubts about the signs of godliness, and were to give some particular sign by which all might know whether or not they were sincerely godly, with such emphatic expressions as these about the man who has such a qualification or mark: #emph[That is the man who is a true saint; that is the very man; by this you may know; this is the thing by which it is made plain who are saints and who are sinners; such men as these are saints indeed.] Should we not look on it as beyond doubt that this was given as a special and eminently distinguishing mark of true godliness? But this is the very case with the sign of grace I am speaking of. God has spoken in his word again and again in this very way about Christian practice. So in John 14: “Whoever has My commandments and keeps them is the one who #emph[loves] Me.” Christ gives this to the disciples in this place not so much to guide them in judging others as to apply to themselves for their own comfort after his departure, as appears from every word of the context. And by the way I would observe that it is not only the emphasis with which Christ speaks that is remarkable, but also his insisting on and repeating the matter so much, as he does in the context. V. 15: “If you#super[+] love Me, you#super[+] will keep My commandments.” Verse 23: “If anyone loves Me, he will keep My word.” And v. 24: “Whoever does not love Me does not keep My words.” And over and over in the next chapter. V. 2: “He cuts off every branch in Me that bears no fruit, and every branch that does bear fruit, He prunes.” Verse 8: “This is to My Father’s glory, that you#super[+] bear much fruit, proving yourselves to be My disciples.” Verse 14: “You#super[+] are My friends if you#super[+] do what I command you#super[+].” We have this mark laid down with the same emphasis again in John 8:31: “If you#super[+] continue in My word, you#super[+] are #emph[truly] My disciples.” And again in 1 John 2:3: “#emph[Hereby] we can be sure that we have come to know Him: if we keep His commandments.” And v. 5: “If anyone keeps His word, the love of God has been #emph[truly] perfected in him. By this we know that we are in Him.” And ch. 3:18, 19: “Let us love … in action and truth. And by this we will know that we belong to the truth.” What is translated #emph[hereby] would have been a little more emphatic if it had been rendered more literally from the original, #emph[by this we know]—. And how evidently holy practice is spoken of as the grand mark of distinction between the children of God and the children of the devil in v. 10 of the same chapter! “#emph[By this] the children of God are distinguished from the children of the devil.” He is speaking of a holy and a wicked practice, as may be seen in all the context, as in v. 3: “Everyone who has this hope in Him purifies himself, just as Christ is pure.” Verses 6, 7, 8, 9, 10: “No one who remains in Him keeps on sinning. No one who continues to sin has seen Him or known Him. Little children, let no one deceive you#super[+]: The one who practices righteousness is righteous, just as Christ is righteous. The one who practices sin is of the devil.—Anyone born of God refuses to practice sin.—Anyone who does not practice righteousness is not of God.” So we have the same emphasis in 2 John 6: “And #emph[this] is love, that we walk according to His commandments.” That is (as we must understand it), #emph[this is the proper evidence of love.] So 1 John 5:3: “For #emph[this] is the love of #emph[God,] that we keep His commandments.” So the apostle #emph[James,] speaking of the proper evidences of true and pure religion, says in Jas 1:27: “Pure and undefiled religion before our God and Father is this: to care for orphans and widows in their distress, and to keep oneself from being polluted by the world.” We have similar emphatic expressions used about the same thing in the Old Testament. Job 28:28: “And He said to man, ‘Behold, the fear of the Lord, that is wisdom, and to turn away from evil is understanding.’ ” Jer 22:15, 16: “Did not your father have food and drink? He administered justice and righteousness.—He took up the cause of the poor and needy. … Is this not what it means to know Me?” declares the #smallcaps[Lord]. Ps 34:11, etc.: “Come, children, listen to me; I will teach you#super[+] the fear of the #smallcaps[Lord].—Keep your tongue from evil and your lips from deceitful speech. Turn away from evil and do good; seek peace and pursue it.” Ps 15 at the beginning: “O #smallcaps[Lord], who may abide in Your tent? Who may dwell on Your holy mountain? He who walks with integrity,” etc. Ps 24:3, 4: “Who may ascend the hill of the #smallcaps[Lord]? Who may stand in His holy place? He who has clean hands and a pure heart,” etc. Ps 119:1: “Blessed are those whose way is blameless, who walk in the Law of the #smallcaps[Lord].” Verse 6: “Then I would not be ashamed when I consider all Your commandments.” Prov 8:13: “To fear the #smallcaps[Lord] is to hate evil.”]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[So Scripture never uses such emphatic expressions about any other signs of hypocrisy and unsoundness of heart as it does about an unholy practice. So Gal 6:7: “Do not be deceived: God is not to be mocked. Whatever a man sows, he will reap in return.” 1 Cor 6:9, 10: “Do not be deceived: Neither the sexually immoral, nor idolaters,” etc., “will inherit the kingdom of God.” Eph 5:5, 6: “For of this you#super[+] can be sure: No immoral, impure,” etc., “has any inheritance in the kingdom of Christ and of God. Let no one deceive you#super[+] with empty words.” 1 John 3:7, 8: “Little children, let no one deceive you#super[+]: The one who practices righteousness is righteous, just as Christ is righteous. The one who practices sin is of the devil.” Chapter 2:4: “If anyone says, ‘I know Him,’ but does not keep His commandments, he is a liar, and the truth is not in him.” And ch. 1:6: “If we say we have fellowship with Him yet walk in the darkness, we lie and do not practice the truth.” Jas 1:26: “If anyone considers himself religious and yet does not bridle his tongue, he deceives his heart and his religion is worthless.” Chapter 3:14, 15: “But if you#super[+] harbor bitter jealousy and selfish ambition in your#super[+] hearts, do not boast in it or deny the truth. Such wisdom does not come from above, but is earthly, unspiritual, demonic.” Ps 125:5:]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[“Those who turn to crooked ways the #smallcaps[Lord] will banish with the evildoers.” Isa 35:8: “And there will be a highway called the Way of Holiness. The unclean will not travel it.” Rev 21:27: “But nothing unclean will ever enter it, nor anyone who practices an abomination or a lie.” And in many places: “Depart from Me; I do not know you#super[+], you#super[+] who work iniquity.”]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Argument] VI. Another thing that makes it evident that holy practice is the chief of all the signs of the sincerity of those who profess, not only to the world but to their own consciences, is that this is the grand evidence that will be used hereafter before the judgment seat of God. His judgment will be governed by it, and the state of every professor of religion will be determined by it beyond change. In the future judgment there will be an open trial of those who profess, and evidences will be used in the judgment. For God’s future judging of men, for their eternal reward or punishment, will not be his trying and finding out the state of men’s hearts and passing judgment on it in his own mind. It will be a declarative judgment, and its end will not be God’s forming a judgment within himself, but the manifestation of his judgment, and of its righteousness, to men’s own consciences and to the world. That is why the day of judgment is called the day “when God’s righteous judgment will be #emph[revealed]” (Rom 2:5). And the end of God’s future trial and judgment of men, as to the part each one in particular is to have in the judgment, will be especially the clear manifestation to his conscience of God’s righteous judgment concerning him, as is plain from Matthew 18:31 to the end; chapter 20:8–15; chapter 22:11, 12, 13; chapter 25:19–30, and verse 35 to the end; Luke 19:15–23. And so, though God needs no means of making the truth evident to himself, yet evidences will be used in his future judging of men. And doubtless the evidences used in their trial will be those best fitted to serve the ends of the judgment, namely, the manifestation of the righteous judgment of God not only to the world but to men’s own consciences. But the Scriptures abundantly teach us that the grand evidences the judge will use in the trial for these ends, by which the judgment of everyone will be governed and the irreversible sentence passed, will be men’s works or practice here in this world. Rev 20:12: “And I saw the dead, great and small, standing before the throne. And there were open books.—And the dead were judged according to their deeds, as recorded in the books.” So verse 13: “The sea gave up its dead, and Death and Hades gave up their dead, and each one was judged according to his deeds.” 2 Cor 5:10: “For we must all appear before the judgment seat of Christ, that each one may receive his due for the things done in the body, whether good or bad.” So men’s practice is the only evidence by which Christ represents the future judgment as governed in the most detailed description of the day of judgment we have in the holy Bible, at the end of Matt 25. See also Rom 2:6–13; Jer 17:10; Job 34:11; Prov 24:12; Jer 32:19; Rev 22:12; Matt 16:27; Rev 2:23; Ezek 33:20; 1 Pet 1:17. At the day of judgment the judge will not (for the conviction of men’s own consciences, and to show them to the world) set about examining men as to the method of their experiences, or set every man to tell his story of the manner of his conversion. Rather, his works will be brought out as evidences of what he is, of what he has done in darkness and in light. Eccl 12:14: “For God will bring every deed into judgment, along with every hidden thing, whether good or evil.” In the trial those who profess will undergo in the future judgment, God will use the same evidences to show them to themselves and to the world as he uses to show them in the temptations or trials of his providence here, namely, their practice in cases where Christ and other things come into actual and immediate competition. At the day of judgment, to show his righteous judgment, God will weigh those who profess in a balance that is visible. And the balance will be the same one he weighs men in now, which has already been described.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[From this we may undoubtedly infer that men’s works (taken in the sense explained) are the highest evidences by which they ought to try themselves. Certainly what our supreme judge will chiefly use to judge us by when we come to stand before him, we should chiefly use to judge ourselves by. #footnote[What God makes the rule of his own judgment, by which he judges every man, is a sure rule for every man to judge himself by. What we will be judged by at the last day is a sure rule to apply to ourselves now. Now he judges us by our obedience and works: he “will repay each one according to his deeds.” Dr. #emph[Preston]’s churches carriage.] If it had not been revealed in what manner, and by what evidence, the judge would proceed with us hereafter, how natural it would be for someone to say: “Oh, that I knew what token God will chiefly look for and insist on in the last and decisive judgment, which he expects all who would then be accepted by him to be able to produce, and by which sentence will be passed; so that I might know what token or evidence especially to look at and seek now, as I would be sure not to fail then.” And since God has so plainly and abundantly revealed what this token or evidence is, surely if we act wisely we will regard it as of the greatest importance.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Now from all that has been said I think it is abundantly clear that Christian practice is the most proper evidence of the gracious sincerity of those who profess, to themselves and to others, and the chief of all the marks of grace: the sign of signs and evidence of evidences, the thing that seals and crowns all other signs.—I would rather have the testimony of my conscience that I have on my side such a saying of my supreme judge as John 14:21, “Whoever has My commandments and keeps them is the one who loves Me,” than the judgment and fullest approval of all the wise, sound and experienced divines who have lived in the last thousand years, after the most exact and critical examination of my experiences as to the manner of my conversion. Not that there are no other good evidences of a state of grace but this. There may be other exercises of grace besides these exercises that produce action, which the saints may have in contemplation, that may be very satisfying to them. But still this is the chief and most proper evidence. There may be several good evidences that a tree is a fig tree, but the highest and most proper evidence of it is that it actually bears figs. It is possible that a man may have a good assurance of a state of grace at his first conversion, before he has had opportunity to gain assurance by this great evidence I am speaking of.—Suppose a man hears that a great treasure is offered him in a distant place, on condition that he prizes it so much as to be willing to leave what he possesses at home and make a journey for it, over the rocks and mountains in the way, to the place where it is. It is possible that the man may be well assured, as soon as the offer is made him, that he values the treasure to the degree spoken of. He may feel within him, beyond all doubt, a willingness to go for the treasure. But still this does not prevent his actually going for it from being the highest and most proper evidence of his being willing, not only to others but to himself. But then, as an evidence to himself, his outward actions and the motions of his body on his journey are not considered alone, apart from the action of his mind, and an inward consciousness of what moves him and of the end he goes for. Otherwise his bodily motion is no evidence to him of his prizing the treasure. It is in this way that Christian practice is the most proper evidence of a saving value set on #emph[the pearl of great price and the treasure hidden in the field.]]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Christian practice is the sign of signs in this sense: that it is the great evidence that confirms and crowns all other signs of godliness. There is no grace of the Spirit of God of whose truth Christian practice is not the most proper evidence. As it is with the members of our bodies and all our tools, the proper proof of their soundness and goodness is in their use, so it is with our graces. They are given to be used in practice, as much as our hands and feet, or the tools we work with, or the weapons we fight with; and the proper trial and proof of them is in their exercise in practice. Most of the things we use are serviceable to us, and so have their serviceableness proved, under some pressure, straining, shaking or collision. So it is with a bow, a sword, an axe, a saw, a cord, a chain, a staff, a foot, a tooth, etc. And those that are so weak as not to bear the strain or pressure we need to put them to are good for nothing. So it is with all the virtues of the mind. Their proper trial and proof is in their being exercised under the temptations and trials God brings us under in the course of his providence, and in their being put to such service as strains hard on the principles of nature.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Practice is the proper proof of the true and saving #emph[knowledge of God,] as appears from the apostle’s words already mentioned: “By this we can be sure that we have come to know Him: if we keep His commandments.” It is useless for us to #emph[profess to know God] if #emph[by our works we deny him] (Titus 1:16). And if we #emph[know God, but do not glorify him as God,] our knowledge will only condemn us and not save us (Rom 1:21). The great mark of the knowledge that saves and makes happy is that it is practical. John 13:17: “If you#super[+] know these things, you#super[+] will be happy if you#super[+] do them.” Job 28:28: “To turn away from evil is understanding.”]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Holy practice is the proper evidence of #emph[repentance.] When the #emph[Jews] professed repentance, when they came confessing their sins to #emph[John,] who preached the baptism of repentance for the forgiveness of sins, he directed them to the right way of getting and showing proper evidences of the truth of their repentance when he said to them, “Produce fruit, then, in keeping with repentance” (Matt 3:8). This agreed with the practice of the apostle #emph[Paul;] see Acts 26:20. Pardon and mercy are again and again promised to the one who has this evidence of true repentance, that he forsakes his sin (Prov 28:13, and Isa 55:7, and many other places).]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Holy practice is the proper evidence of a #emph[saving faith.] It is evident that the apostle #emph[James] speaks of works as what eminently justifies faith, or (which is the same thing) justifies those who profess faith, and vindicates and shows the sincerity of their profession, not only to the world but to their own consciences. This is evident from the instance he gives of #emph[Abraham] (Jas 2:21–24).—And in verses 20 and 26 he speaks of the practical and working nature of faith as its very life and soul, in the same way that the active nature and substance in the body of a man is its life and soul. And if so, doubtless practice is the proper evidence of the life and soul of true faith, by which it is distinguished from a dead faith. For doubtless practice is the most proper evidence of a practical nature, and working the most proper evidence of a working nature.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Practice is the best evidence of a saving #emph[belief of the truth.] It is spoken of as the proper evidence of the #emph[truth’s being in] a professing Christian #emph[that he walks in the truth.] 3 John 3: “For I was overjoyed when the brothers came and testified of the truth that is in you, just as you continue to walk in the truth.”]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Practice is the most proper evidence of a true #emph[coming to Christ,] and #emph[accepting] him and #emph[embracing him.] A true and saving coming to Christ is (as Christ often teaches) a coming in such a way as to forsake all for him. And as was observed before, to forsake all for Christ in heart is the same thing as to have a heart actually to forsake all. But the proper evidence of having a heart actually to forsake all is actually to forsake all, as far as we are called to it. If a prince seeks the hand of a woman in a far country, asking her to forsake her own people and her father’s house and come to him to be his bride, the proper evidence that her heart complies with the king’s suit is her actually forsaking her own people and her father’s house and coming to him. By this her compliance with the king’s suit is made perfect, in the same sense in which the apostle #emph[James] says #emph[faith was perfected by works.] #footnote[Our real taking of Christ appears in our actions and works. Isa 1:19: “If you#super[+] consent and obey, you#super[+] will eat the best of the land.” That is, if you will consent to take Jehovah for your Lord and King. #emph[If you give consent:] there is the first thing. But that is not enough: #emph[but if you also obey.] The truth of the consent that stands in the inward act of the mind will be seen in your obedience, in the acts of your lives. #emph[If you consent and obey, you will eat the best of the land;] that is, you will take of all he has that is fitting for you. For then you are married to him in truth, and have a share in all his goods. Dr. #emph[Preston] Church’s carriage.] Christ promises us eternal life on condition of our coming to him. But it is such a coming as he directed the young man to, who came to ask #emph[what he must do to obtain eternal life.] Christ told him to #emph[go and sell all that he had, and come to him and follow him.] If he had consented in his heart to the proposal (and so had come to Christ in his heart), the proper evidence of it would have been his doing it; and in that his coming to Christ would have been made perfect. When Christ called #emph[Levi] the tax collector, as he sat at the tax booth in the midst of his worldly gains, #emph[Levi]’s embrace, in his heart, of this invitation of his Savior to come to him was shown and made perfect by his actually getting up, leaving all and following him (Luke 5:27, 28). Christ and other things are set before us together, for us practically to hold to one and forsake the other. In such a case a practical holding to Christ is a practical #emph[acceptance] of Christ, as much as a beggar’s reaching out his hand and taking a gift that is offered is his practical acceptance of the gift. Indeed, the act of the soul that is in holding to Christ in practice is itself the most perfect coming of the soul to Christ.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Practice is the most proper evidence of #emph[trusting in Christ for salvation.] The proper meaning of the word #emph[trust,] in its more ordinary use, both in common speech and in the holy Scriptures, is the emboldening and encouraging of a person’s mind to run some risk in practice, or in something he does, on the credit of another’s sufficiency and faithfulness. And so the proper evidence of his trusting is #emph[the risk he runs in what he does.] A man is not properly said to run any risk in dependence on anything if he does nothing on that dependence, or if his practice is no different from what it would be if he had no dependence. For a man to run a risk in dependence on another is for him to do something from that dependence by which he seems to expose himself, and which he would not do if it were not for that dependence. And so it is in complying with the difficulties and seeming dangers of Christian practice, in dependence on Christ’s sufficiency and faithfulness to bestow eternal life, that persons are said to venture themselves on Christ and trust in him for happiness and life. They depend on such promises as Matt 10:39: “Whoever loses his life for My sake will find it.” And so they part with all, and venture their all, in dependence on Christ’s sufficiency and truth. And this is the Scripture idea of trusting in Christ in the exercise of a saving faith in him. In this way #emph[Abraham,] the father of believers, trusted in Christ, and by faith forsook his own country, relying on the covenant of grace God established with him (Heb 11:8, 9). In this way also #emph[Moses, by faith, refused to be called the son of Pharaoh’s daughter, choosing to suffer oppression with the people of God rather than to experience the fleeting enjoyment of sin] (Heb 11:24, etc.). So #emph[by faith] others exposed themselves to be #emph[stoned] and #emph[sawed in two,] or #emph[put to death by the sword;] they endured the #emph[trial of cruel mocking and flogging, chains and imprisonment, and went around in sheepskins and goatskins, destitute, oppressed, mistreated.] And in this sense the apostle #emph[Paul] by faith trusted in Christ and committed himself to him, venturing himself and his whole interest in dependence on the ability and faithfulness of his Redeemer, under great persecutions and in suffering the loss of all things. 2 Tim 1:12: “For this reason, even though I suffer as I do, I am not ashamed; for I know whom I have believed, and I am convinced that He is able to guard what I have entrusted to Him for that day.”]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Suppose a man had word brought to him from the king of a distant land that he intended to make him his heir. If on receiving the news he immediately leaves his native land and friends and all he has in the world to go to that country, in dependence on what he hears, then he may be said to venture himself, and all he has in the world, on it. But if he only sits still and hopes for the promised benefit, inwardly pleasing himself with the thought of it, he cannot properly be said to venture himself on it. He runs no risk in the case. He does nothing different from what he would do if he had received no such news, nothing by which he would be exposed to any suffering in case it all failed. So he who, on the credit of what he hears of a future world, and in dependence on the report of the gospel concerning life and immortality, forsakes all, or at least does so as far as there is occasion, making everything give way entirely to his eternal interest: he, and he only, may properly be said to venture himself on the report of the gospel. And this is the proper evidence of a true trust in Christ for salvation.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Practice is the proper evidence of a #emph[gracious love,] both to God and to men. The texts that plainly teach this have been mentioned so often already that there is no need to repeat them.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Practice is the proper evidence of #emph[humility.] The expression and manifestation of humility of heart that God speaks of as its great expression, the one he insists on, is the one we should look on as its proper expression and manifestation. But this is #emph[walking humbly.] Mic 6:8: “He has shown you, O man, what is good. And what does the #smallcaps[Lord] require of you but to act justly, to love mercy, and to #emph[walk humbly] with your God?”]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[This is also the proper evidence of the true #emph[fear of God.] Prov 8:13.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[“To fear the #smallcaps[Lord] is to hate evil.” Ps 34:11, etc.: “Come, children, listen to me; I will teach you#super[+] the fear of the #smallcaps[Lord].—Keep your tongue from evil and your lips from deceitful speech. Turn away from evil and do good; seek peace and pursue it.” Prov 3:7: “Fear the #smallcaps[Lord] and turn away from evil.” Prov 16:6: “By the fear of the #smallcaps[Lord] one turns aside from evil.” Job 1:8: “Have you considered My servant #emph[Job?]—a man who is blameless and upright, who fears God and shuns evil.” Chapter 2:3: “Have you considered My servant #emph[Job?]—a man who is blameless and upright, who fears God and shuns evil. He still retains his integrity, even though you incited Me against him.” Ps 36:1: “An oracle is in my heart regarding the transgression of the wicked man: There is no fear of God before his eyes.”]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[So practice, in repaying according to the benefits received, is the proper evidence of true #emph[thankfulness.] Ps 116:12: “How can I repay the #smallcaps[Lord] for all His goodness to me?” 2 Chr 32:25: “But … #emph[Hezekiah] did not repay the favor shown to him.” In the 50th Psalm, paying our vows to God and ordering our way rightly seem to be spoken of as the proper expression and evidence of true thankfulness. Verse 14: “Sacrifice a thank offering to God, and fulfill your vows to the Most High.” Verse 23: “He who sacrifices a thank offering honors Me, and to him who rights his way, I will show the salvation of God.”]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[So the proper evidence of #emph[gracious desires and longings,] and what distinguishes them from those that are false and empty, is that they are not idle wishes and would-be wishes, like #emph[Balaam]’s, but are effectual in practice, stirring persons up to seek earnestly and thoroughly the things they long for. Ps 27:4: “One thing I have desired of the #smallcaps[Lord]; that will I seek.” Ps 63:1, 2: “O God, You are my God. Earnestly I seek You; my soul thirsts for You. My body yearns for You in a dry and weary land without water. … Your power and glory.” Verse 8: “My soul follows hard after You.” Song 1:4: “Draw me after you—let us run!”]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Practice is the proper evidence of a gracious #emph[hope.] 1 John 3:3: “Everyone who has this hope in Him purifies himself, just as Christ is pure.” Patient continuance in doing good, through the difficulties and trials of the Christian course, is often mentioned as the proper expression and fruit of a Christian hope. 1 Thess 1:3: “Continually recalling … your#super[+] work of faith, your#super[+] labor of love, and your#super[+] patience of hope.” 1 Pet 1:13, 14: “Therefore prepare your#super[+] minds for action. Be sober-minded. Set your#super[+] hope fully on the grace to be given you#super[+] at the revelation of Jesus Christ. As obedient children,” etc. Ps 119:166: “I hope for Your salvation, O #smallcaps[Lord], and I carry out Your commandments.” Ps 78:7: “that they should set their hope in God, not forgetting His works, but keeping His commandments.”]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[A cheerful practice of our duty and doing of the will of God is the proper evidence of a truly #emph[holy joy.] Isa 64:5: “You welcome those who gladly do right.” Ps 119:111, 112: “Your testimonies are my heritage forever, for they are the joy of my heart. I have inclined my heart to perform Your statutes, even to the very end.” Verse 14: “I rejoice in the way of Your testimonies as much as in all riches.” 1 Cor 13:6: “Love takes no pleasure in evil, but rejoices in the truth.” 2 Cor 8:2: “Their abundant joy … overflowed into rich generosity.”]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Practice is also the proper evidence of Christian #emph[fortitude.] The trial of a good soldier is not in his chimney corner but on the field of battle (1 Cor 9:25, 26; 2 Tim 2:3, 4, 5).]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And as the fruit of holy practice is the chief evidence of the truth of grace, so the degree to which experiences influence a person’s practice is the surest evidence of the degree of what is spiritual and divine in his experiences. Whatever claims persons may make to great discoveries, great love and joys, they are to be regarded no further than they influence their practice. Allowances must indeed be made for the natural temper. But that does not prevent the degree of grace from being justly measured by the degree of the effect in practice. For the effect of grace is as great, and the change as remarkable, in a person of a very bad natural temper as in another. Although a person of such a temper will not behave as well as another with the same degree of grace, the difference from what he was before conversion may be as great, because a person of a good natural temper did not behave so badly before conversion.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[So I have tried to set out the evidence that Christian practice is #emph[the chief] of all the signs of saving grace. And before I conclude this discourse, I would say something briefly in answer to two objections that some may possibly make against what has been said on this head.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Objection] 1. Some may be ready to say that this seems contrary to the opinion so widely received among good people, that those who profess should judge their state chiefly by their inward experience, and that spiritual experiences are the main evidences of true grace.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[I answer: it is doubtless a true opinion, and justly much received among good people, that those who profess should chiefly judge their state by their experience. But it is a great mistake to think that what has been said is at all contrary to that opinion. That the chief sign of grace to the consciences of Christians is Christian practice, in the sense explained, and according to what has been shown to be the true idea of Christian practice, is not at all inconsistent with Christian experience being the chief evidence of grace. Christian or holy practice is spiritual practice. And that is not the motion of a body that does not know how, or when, or why it moves. Spiritual practice in man is the practice of a spirit and body jointly, or the practice of a spirit animating, commanding and moving a body to which it is united, and over which the Creator has given it power. And so the main thing in this holy practice is the holy acts of the mind, directing and governing the motions of the body. And the motions of the body are to be looked on as belonging to Christian practice only secondarily, and as they depend on and follow from the acts of the soul. The exercises of grace that Christians find, or are conscious of, within themselves are what they #emph[experience] within themselves, and so Christian experience lies in this. And this Christian experience consists as much in the working exercises of grace in the will that are immediately concerned in managing the behavior of the body as in other exercises. These inward exercises are no less a part of Christian experience because outward behavior is immediately connected with them. A strong act of love to God is no less a part of spiritual experience because it is the act that immediately produces and brings about some self-denying and costly outward action that is much to the honor and glory of God.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[To speak of Christian experience and practice as if they were two things properly and entirely distinct is to make a distinction without consideration or reason. It is true that not all Christian experience is properly called practice; but all Christian practice is properly experience. And the distinction made between them is not only an unreasonable but an unscriptural distinction. Holy practice is one kind or part of Christian experience, and both reason and Scripture represent it as its chief, most important and most distinguishing part. So it is represented in Jeremiah 22:15, 16: “Did not your father have food and drink? He administered justice and righteousness.—He took up the cause of the poor and needy. … Is this not what it means to know Me?” declares the #smallcaps[Lord]. Our inward acquaintance with God surely belongs under the head of experiential religion; but God represents this as consisting chiefly in the experience there is in holy practice. So the exercises of the graces of the love of God and the fear of God are a part of experiential religion; but Scripture represents these as consisting chiefly in practice, in the texts mentioned before. 1 John 5:3: “This is the love of God, that we keep His commandments.” 2 John 6: “This is love, that we walk according to His commandments.” Ps 34:11, etc.: “Come, children, … I will teach you#super[+] the fear of the #smallcaps[Lord].—Turn away from evil and do good.” Such experiences as these were what #emph[Hezekiah] chiefly took comfort in on his sickbed, when he said, “Please, O #smallcaps[Lord], remember how I have walked before You faithfully and with wholehearted devotion.” #emph[And] such experiences as these are what the psalmist chiefly insists on in the 119th Psalm and elsewhere. Such experiences as these are what the apostle #emph[Paul] mainly insists on when he speaks of his experiences in his epistles. So Rom 1:9: “God, whom I serve with my spirit in preaching the gospel of His Son, is my witness.”—2 Cor 1:12: “And this is our boast: Our conscience testifies that we have conducted ourselves in the world … in the grace of God.” Chapter 4:13: “And in keeping with what is written: ‘I believed, therefore I have spoken,’ we who have the same spirit of faith also believe and therefore speak.” Chapter 5:7: “For we walk by faith, not by sight.” Verse 14: “For Christ’s love compels us.” Chapter 6:4–7: “As servants of God we commend ourselves in every way: in great endurance; in troubles, hardships, and calamities;—in labor, sleepless nights, and hunger; in purity, knowledge, patience, and kindness; in the Holy Spirit and in sincere love;—in the power of God.” Gal 2:20: “I have been crucified with Christ, and I no longer live, but Christ lives in me. The life I live in the body, I live by faith in the Son of God.” Phil 3:7, 8: “But whatever was gain to me I count as loss for the sake of Christ. More than that, I count all things as loss compared to the surpassing excellence of knowing Christ Jesus my Lord. … I consider them rubbish, that I may gain Christ.” Col 1:29: “To this end I also labor, striving with all His energy working powerfully within me.” 1 Thess 2:2: “In the face of strong opposition, we were bold in our God to speak to you#super[+] the gospel of God.” Verses 8, 9, 10: “We cared so deeply that we were delighted to share with you#super[+] not only the gospel of God, but our own lives as well. That is how beloved you#super[+] have become to us. Surely you#super[+] recall, brothers, our labor and toil. We worked night and day.—You#super[+] are witnesses, and so is God, of how holy, righteous, and blameless our conduct was among you#super[+].” And it was such experiences as these that this blessed apostle chiefly comforted himself in considering when he was going to martyrdom (2 Tim 4:6, 7): “For I am already being poured out like a drink offering, and the time of my departure is at hand. I have fought the good fight, I have finished the race, I have kept the faith.”]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And not only does the most important and distinguishing part of Christian experience lie in spiritual practice; but such is the nature of the sort of exercises of grace in which spiritual practice consists that nothing is so properly called by the name of #emph[experiential religion.] For the experience that is in those exercises of grace that are found, and prove effectual, at the very point of trial, where God proves which we will actually hold to, whether Christ or our lusts, is, as has already been shown, the proper #emph[experiment] of the truth and power of our godliness. In it its victorious power and efficacy, in producing its proper effect and reaching its end, is found #emph[by experience.] This is properly Christian experience, in which the saints have opportunity to see by actual #emph[experience and trial] whether or not they have a heart to do the will of God and to forsake other things for Christ. As the philosophy that brings opinions and ideas to the test of fact is called experimental philosophy, so the religion that brings religious affections and intentions to the same test is properly called experimental religion.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[There is a sort of outward religious practice in which there is no inward experience; God takes no account of it, but counts it good for nothing. And there is what is called experience that is without practice, neither accompanied nor followed by Christian behavior; and this is worse than nothing. Many persons seem to have very wrong ideas of Christian experience and of spiritual light and discoveries. Whenever a person finds within him a heart to treat God as God at the time he is put to the trial, and finds his disposition effectual in the experiment, that is the most proper and most distinguishing experience. And to have at such a time the sense of divine things, the apprehension of the truth, importance and excellence of the things of religion, which then sways and prevails and governs his heart and hands: this is the most excellent spiritual light, and these are the most distinguishing discoveries. Religion consists much in holy affection; but the exercises of affection that most distinguish true religion are these practical exercises. Friendship between earthly friends consists much in affection; yet the strong exercises of affection that actually carry them through fire and water for each other are the highest evidences of true friendship.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[There is nothing in what has been said contrary to what some sound divines assert when they say that there are no sure evidences of grace but the acts of grace. For that does not prevent these working, productive acts, the exercises of grace that are effectual in practice, from being the highest evidences, above all other kinds of acts of grace. Nor does it prevent the evidence from being heightened still further when there are many of these acts and exercises following one another in a course, under various trials of every kind, as one act confirms another. A man may have good evidence of his neighbor’s presence by seeing him once; but by seeing him day after day, and conversing with him regularly in various circumstances, the evidence is established. When the disciples first saw Christ after his resurrection, they had good evidence that he was alive. But by conversing with him for forty days, and by his #emph[showing himself alive to them with many convincing proofs,] they had still higher evidence. #footnote[The more these visible exercises of grace are renewed, the more certain you will be. The more often these actings are renewed, the more lasting and confirmed your assurance will be. A man who has been sure of such visible exercises of grace may soon afterward be in doubt whether he was not mistaken. But when such actings are renewed again and again, he grows more settled and established about his good state. If a man sees a thing once, that makes him sure. But if afterward he fears he was deceived, when he comes to see it again he is surer that he was not mistaken. If a man reads certain passages in a book, he is sure it is so. Some months later some people may argue him down, saying he was mistaken, so that he questions it himself; but when he looks and reads it again, he is abundantly confirmed. The more men’s grace is multiplied, the more their peace is multiplied. 2 Pet 1:2: “Grace and peace be multiplied to you#super[+] through the knowledge of God and of Jesus our Lord.” #emph[Stoddard’s] way to know sincerity and hypocrisy.]]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The witness or seal of the Spirit that we read of doubtless consists in the effect of the Spirit of God in the heart, in the implanting and exercises of grace there, and so it consists in experience. And it is also beyond doubt that this seal of the Spirit is the highest kind of evidence of their adoption that the saints ever obtain. But in these exercises of grace in practice that have been spoken of, God gives witness and sets his seal in the most conspicuous, eminent and evident way. The experience of the Christian church has abundantly found it to be true in fact that Christ commonly gives his saints by his Spirit the greatest and most joyful evidences of their sonship in those effectual exercises of grace under trials that have been spoken of. This is plain in the full assurance and unspeakable joys of many of the martyrs. It agrees with 1 Pet 4:14: “If you#super[+] are insulted for the name of Christ, you#super[+] are blessed, because the Spirit of glory and of God rests on you#super[+].” And with Romans 5:2, 3: “We rejoice in the hope of the glory of God. Not only that, but we also rejoice in our sufferings.” And it agrees with what the apostle #emph[Paul] often declares of what he experienced in his trials. And when the apostle #emph[Peter] in my text speaks of the #emph[joy unspeakable and full of glory] that the Christians he wrote to experienced, he has in view what they found under persecution, as appears from the context. Christ’s manifesting himself in this way as the friend and Savior of his saints, who hold to him under trials, seems to have been represented of old by his coming and manifesting himself to #emph[Shadrach, Meshach] and #emph[Abednego] in the furnace. And when the apostle speaks of the witness of the Spirit in Romans 8:15, 16, 17, he has more immediately in view what the Christians experienced in their exercises of love to God in suffering persecution, as is plain from the context. In the preceding verses he is encouraging the Christian #emph[Romans] under their sufferings, telling them that though their bodies are dead because of sin, yet they will be raised to life again. But it is especially plain from the verse immediately following, v. 18: “I consider that our present sufferings are not comparable to the glory that will be revealed in us.” So the apostle evidently has their persecutions in view in all he says to the end of the chapter. So when the apostle speaks of the #emph[earnest of the Spirit] that God had given him, in 2 Corinthians 5:5, the context plainly shows that he has in view what was given him in his great trials and sufferings. And in the promise of #emph[the white stone and the new name to the one who overcomes] (Rev 2:17), it is evident that Christ has special regard to a benefit Christians would obtain by overcoming in the trial they had in that day of persecution. This appears from v. 13, and many other passages in this epistle to the seven churches of #emph[Asia.]]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Objection] 2. Some may also be ready to object against what has been said of Christian practice being the chief evidence of the truth of grace that this is a #emph[legal] doctrine: that making practice a thing of such great importance in religion magnifies #emph[works,] and tends to lead men to make too much of their #emph[own doings,] lessening the glory of free grace, and does not seem to agree well with the great gospel doctrine of #emph[justification by faith alone.]]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But this objection is altogether without reason. In what way is it inconsistent with the freeness of God’s grace that holy practice should be a sign of God’s grace? It is our works being the #emph[price] of God’s favor, and not their being the #emph[sign] of it, that is inconsistent with the freeness of that favor. Surely the beggar’s looking on the money in his hands as a #emph[sign] of the kindness of the one who gave it to him is in no respect inconsistent with the freeness of that kindness. It is his having money in his hand as the price of a benefit that is inconsistent with the free kindness of the giver. The idea of the freeness of the grace of God to sinners, as it is revealed and taught in the gospel, is not that no holy and lovely qualifications or actions in us will be a fruit, and so a sign, of that grace. It is that it is not the worthiness or loveliness of any qualification or action of ours that recommends us to that grace; that kindness is shown to the unworthy and unlovely; that there is great excellence in the benefit bestowed, and no excellence in the one who receives it as the price of it; that goodness goes forth and flows out from the fullness of God’s nature, the fullness of the fountain of good, without any loveliness in the object to draw it. And this is the idea of justification without works (as this doctrine is taught in Scripture): that it is not the worthiness or loveliness of our works, or of anything in us, that is in any way accepted by God as a #emph[balance] for the guilt of sin, or as a recommendation of sinners to his acceptance as heirs of life. So we are justified only by the righteousness of Christ, and not by our own righteousness. And when works are set against faith in this matter, and it is said that we are justified by faith and not by works, what is meant is that it is not the worthiness or loveliness of our works, or of anything in us, that recommends us to a share in Christ and his benefits, but that we have this share only by faith, by our souls’ receiving Christ, or clinging to him and embracing him. But that the worthiness or loveliness of nothing in us recommends us to a share in Christ and brings us into it is no argument that nothing in us is a sign of a share in Christ.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[If the doctrines of free grace and justification by faith alone are inconsistent with the importance of holy practice as a sign of grace, then they are equally inconsistent with the importance of anything whatever in us as a sign of grace: any holiness, or any grace in us, or any of our experiences or religion. For it is as contrary to the doctrines of free grace and justification by faith alone that any of these should be the righteousness we are justified by as that holy practice should be. It is with holy works as it is with holy qualifications. It is as inconsistent with the freeness of gospel grace that a title to salvation should be given to men for the loveliness of any of their holy qualifications as that it should be given for the holiness of their works. It is inconsistent with the gospel doctrine of free grace that a share in Christ and his benefits should be given for the loveliness of a man’s true holiness: for the loveliness of his renewed, sanctified, heavenly heart, his love to God and likeness to God, or his experience of joy in the Holy Spirit, self-emptiness, a spirit that exalts Christ above all and gives all glory to him, and a heart devoted to him. I say, it is inconsistent with the gospel doctrine of free grace that a title to Christ’s benefits should be given out of regard to the loveliness of any of these, or that any of these should be our righteousness in the matter of justification. And yet this does not lessen the importance of these things as evidences of a share in Christ. It is just the same with holy actions and works. To make light of works because we are not justified by works is in effect the same thing as to make light of all religion, all grace and holiness, indeed true evangelical holiness, and all gracious experience. For all of it is included when Scripture says we are not justified by works. For by works in this case is meant all our own righteousness, religion or holiness, and everything that #emph[is in us:] all the good we do and all the good we are conscious of; all outward acts, and all inward acts and exercises of grace; all experiences; all the holy and heavenly things in which the life and power and very essence of religion consist; all the great things Christ and his apostles mainly insisted on in their preaching, and tried to promote as of the greatest consequence in the hearts and lives of men; all good dispositions, exercises and qualifications of every kind whatever; and even faith itself, considered as a part of our holiness. For we are justified by none of these things; and if we were, we would in a Scripture sense be justified by works. And so if it is not legal, and contrary to the evangelical doctrine of justification without works, to insist on any of these as of great importance as evidences of a share in Christ, then neither is it legal to insist on the importance of holy practice. It would be legal to suppose that holy practice justifies by bringing us to a title to Christ’s benefits as the price of it, or as recommending us to it by its preciousness or excellence. But it is not legal to suppose that holy practice justifies the sincerity of a believer, as its proper evidence. The apostle #emph[James] did not think it legal to say in this sense that #emph[Abraham our father was justified by works.] The Spirit who inspired the Scripture did not think the great importance and absolute necessity of holy practice in this respect to be inconsistent with the freeness of grace, for it commonly teaches them both together. So in Revelation 21:6, 7 God says, “To the thirsty I will give freely from the spring of the water of life,” and then adds in the very next words, “The one who overcomes will inherit all things,” as though behaving well in the Christian race and warfare were the condition of the promise. So in the next chapter, in the 14th and 15th verses, Christ says, “Blessed are those who do His commandments, so that they may have the right to the tree of life and may enter the city by its gates,” and then declares in the 15th verse #emph[how those of a wicked practice will be shut out.] And yet in the next two verses he gives out with very great solemnity an invitation to all to come and take of the water of life freely: “I am the Root and the Offspring of #emph[David,] the bright Morning Star. The Spirit and the bride say, ‘Come!’ Let the one who hears say, ‘Come!’ And let the one who is thirsty come, and the one who desires the water of life drink freely.” So ch. 3:20, 21: “Behold, I stand at the door and knock. If anyone hears My voice and opens the door, I will come in and dine with him, and he with Me.” But then it is added in the next words, “To the one who overcomes, I will grant the right to sit with Me on My throne.” And in the great invitation of Christ at the end of Matt 11, “Come to Me, all you#super[+] who are weary and burdened, and I will give you#super[+] rest,” Christ adds in the next words, “Take My yoke upon you#super[+] and learn from Me; for I am gentle and humble in heart, and you#super[+] will find rest for your#super[+] souls. For My yoke is easy and My burden is light,” as though taking up the burden of Christ’s service and imitating his example were necessary for obtaining the promised rest. So in the great invitation to sinners to accept free grace in Isa 55: “Come, all you#super[+] who are thirsty, come to the waters; and you#super[+] without money, come, buy, and eat! Come, buy wine and milk without money and without cost!” Even there, in the continuation of the same invitation, the sinner’s forsaking his wicked practice is spoken of as necessary for obtaining mercy. Verse 7: “Let the wicked man forsake his own way and the unrighteous man his own thoughts; let him return to the #smallcaps[Lord], that He may have compassion, and to our God, for He will freely pardon.” So the riches of divine grace in the justification of sinners are set forth together with the necessity of holy practice in Isa 1:15, etc.: “Wash and cleanse yourselves. Remove your#super[+] evil deeds from My sight. Stop doing evil! Learn to do right; seek justice and correct the oppressor. Defend the fatherless and plead the case of the widow.” “Come now, let us reason together,” says the #smallcaps[Lord]. “Though your#super[+] sins are like scarlet, they will be as white as snow; though they are as red as crimson, they will become like wool.” And in the most solemn invitation of wisdom in Prov 9, after it is described what great provision is made, and how all things were ready, the house built, the animals slaughtered, the wine mixed and the table set, and the messengers sent out to invite the guests, we then have the free invitation, vv. 4, 5, 6: “ ‘Whoever is simple, let him turn in here!’ she says to him who lacks judgment” (that is, who has no righteousness). “ ‘Come, eat my bread and drink the wine I have mixed.’ ” But then in the next breath it follows, “ ‘Leave your folly behind, and you will live; walk in the way of understanding,’ ” as though forsaking sin and going in the way of holiness were necessary for life. So the freeness of grace and the necessity of holy practice, which Scripture joins together in this way again and again, are not inconsistent with one another. Nor does it at all lessen the honor and importance of faith that the exercises and effects of faith in practice should be counted its chief signs, any more than it lessens the importance of life that action and motion are counted its chief signs.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[So in what has been said of the importance of holy practice as the main sign of sincerity there is nothing legal; nothing that takes away from the freedom and sovereignty of gospel grace; nothing in the least clashing with the gospel doctrine of justification by faith alone, without the works of the law; nothing in the least tending to lessen the glory of the Mediator and our dependence on his righteousness; nothing infringing on the special prerogatives of faith in the matter of our salvation; nothing in any way detracting from the glory of God and his mercy, or exalting man, or lessening his dependence and obligation. So if any are against such an importance of holy practice as has been spoken of, it can only be from a senseless aversion to the letters and sound of the word #emph[works.] For there is no reason in the world that can be given for it which may not be given with equal force for having an aversion to the words holiness, godliness, grace, religion, experience, and even faith itself. For to make a righteousness of any of these is as legal, and as inconsistent with the way of the new covenant, as to make a righteousness of holy practice. #footnote[You say you know Christ, and the love and goodwill of Christ toward you, and that he is the propitiation for your sins. How do you know this? “If anyone says, ‘I know Him,’ but does not keep His commandments, he is a liar” (1 John 2:4). True, some might reply, he who does not keep the commands of Christ has in this a sure evidence that he does not know him and is not united to him; but is it any evidence that we do know him, and that we are united to him, if we do keep his commandments? Yes indeed, says the apostle: “By this we can be sure that we have come to know Him: if we keep His commandments.” And again, v. 5: “By this we know that we are in Him.” What can be plainer? What vanity it is to say that this is running on a covenant of works!—O beloved, it is a sad thing to hear such questions, and such cold answers too: that sanctification possibly may be an evidence. May be? Is it not certain? Surely to deny it is as bad as to affirm that God’s own promises of favor are not sure evidences of it, and so that they are lies and untruths.—Our Savior, who was no legal preacher, pronounces, and so gives evidence of, blessedness by eight or nine promises expressly made to such persons as had inherent graces (Matt 5:3, 4, etc.). #emph[Shepard]’s sound believer. P. 221, 222, 223.]]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[It is greatly to the hurt of religion for persons to make light of, and insist little on, the things Scripture insists on most as of the most importance in the evidence of our share in Christ (supposing that to lay weight on these things is legal, an old-covenant way); and so to neglect the exercises and effectual workings of grace in practice, and to insist almost wholly on discoveries, and on the method and manner of the immanent exercises of conscience and grace in contemplation, depending on an ability to make fine distinctions in these matters, and on a skill in discerning them accurately, from philosophy or experience. It is useless to look for any better or further signs than the ones the Scriptures have most expressly mentioned and most often insisted on as signs of godliness. Those who claim a greater accuracy in giving signs, or claim by their extraordinary experience or insight into the nature of things to give more distinguishing marks, which will search out and expose the hypocrite more thoroughly, are only subtle in darkening their own minds and the minds of others. Their refinements and fine discernings are in God’s sight only refined foolishness and shrewd delusion. The words of #emph[Agur] apply here, Prov 30:5, 6: “Every word of God is flawless; He is a shield to those who take refuge in Him. Do not add to His words, lest He rebuke you and prove you a liar.” Our wisdom and discernment concerning the hearts of men is not much to be trusted. We can see only a little way into the nature of the soul and the depths of man’s heart. The ways in which persons’ affections may be moved without any supernatural influence are so many; the natural springs of the affections are so various and so secret; so many things often have a joint influence on the affections (the imagination, and that in innumerable and unsearchable ways; natural temper; education; the common influences of the Spirit of God; a surprising coming together of moving circumstances; an extraordinary coincidence of things in the course of men’s thoughts; together with the subtle management of invisible malicious spirits) that no philosophy or experience will ever be enough to guide us safely through this labyrinth and maze unless we closely follow the thread God has given us in his word. God knows his own reasons why he insists on some things, and plainly sets them out as the things we should try ourselves by, rather than others. Perhaps it is because he knows that these things are accompanied by less perplexity, and that we are less liable to be deceived by them than by others. He knows our nature best. He knows the nature and manner of his own workings, and he knows best the way of our safety. He knows what allowances to make for different states of his church, and different tempers of particular persons, and varieties in the manner of his own workings; how far nature may resemble grace, and how far nature may be mixed with grace; what affections may arise from imagination, and how far imagination may be mixed with spiritual illumination. And so it is our wisdom not to take his work out of his hands, but to follow him, and to lay the weight of our judgment of ourselves where he has directed us. If we do otherwise, no wonder if we are bewildered, confounded and fatally deluded. But if we had got into the way of looking chiefly at the things Christ and his apostles and prophets chiefly insisted on, and so, in judging ourselves and others, chiefly regarding the practical exercises and effects of grace, while not neglecting other things, it would have many happy consequences. It would above all things tend to the conviction of deluded hypocrites, and to prevent the delusion of those whose hearts were never brought to a thorough compliance with the strait and narrow way that leads to life. It would tend to deliver us from innumerable perplexities arising from the various inconsistent schemes there are about methods and steps of experience. It would greatly tend to keep professing Christians from neglecting strictness of life, and to promote their commitment and earnestness in their Christian walk. It would become the custom for men to show their Christianity more by a lovely, distinguished behavior than by an abundant and excessive declaring of their experiences. We would get into the way of appearing lively in religion more by being lively in the service of God and of our generation than by the liveliness and forwardness of our tongues, and by making a business of proclaiming on the housetops with our mouths the holy and eminent acts and exercises of our own hearts. Christians who are close friends would talk together of their experiences and comforts in a way that better becomes Christian humility and modesty, and more to each other’s profit, their tongues not running ahead of their hands and feet but rather going behind them, after the prudent example of the blessed apostle (2 Cor 12:6). Many occasions of spiritual pride would be cut off, and so a great door shut against the devil. A great many of the main stumbling blocks against experiential and powerful religion would be removed. And religion would be declared and shown in such a way that, instead of hardening onlookers and greatly promoting unbelief and atheism, it would above all things tend to convince men that there is a reality in religion, and greatly awaken them and win them, by convincing their consciences of the importance and excellence of religion. So the light of those who profess would so shine before men that others, seeing their good works, would glorify their Father in heaven.]]
